pair_id,registered_endpoint,published_endpoint,similarity_score,ssi,routing,llm_model,llm_switch_type,llm_reasoning,llm_confidence,llm_comparability,llm_flag,llm_disclosed_exploratory,llm_results_driven,llm_switch_forms,llm_confidence_score,llm_classification_label,llm_primary_endpoint_match,llm_published_primary_extracted,llm_actual_change_evidence,llm_missing_detail_only,llm_adjudication_guardrail,llm_endpoint_comparisons,human_reviewed,human_decision,human_final_class,human_poolable,override_reason,reviewer_initials,review_timestamp,pipeline_version,created_at
NCT00493649_24007746,Disease-free Survival (DFS) Rate at 2 Years in TOP2A-amplified and in TOP2A-nonamplified HER2+ ESBC Patients Treated With TC+H. [Time Frame: 2 years],"493 patients were enrolled between June 15, 2007, and Aug 5, 2009. After a median follow-up of 36·1 months (IQR 35·5-36·7), 2-year DFS was 97·8% (95% CI 94·2-99·2) and 2-year overall survival was 99·5% (95% CI 96·2-99·9) for the 190 patients with TOP2A-amplified disease; 2-year DFS was 97·9% (95% CI 94·9-99·1) and 2-year overall survival was 98·8% (95% CI 96·2-99·6) for the 248 patients with TOP2A-non-amplified disease; 55 patients were not assessable for TOP2A status. In the 486 patients who received at least one dose of study drug, the most common adverse events of any grade were fatigue (284 patients, 58·4%), neutropenia (250, 51·4%), and nausea (217, 44·7%). The most common grade 3-4 toxic effects were neutropenia (229, 47·1%), febrile neutropenia (30, 6·2%), fatigue (21, 4·3%), and diarrhoea (16, 3·3%). Cardiac dysfunction occurred in 29 (6·0%) patients (12 [2·5%] grade 1, 15 [3·1%] grade 2, and two [0·4%] grade 3). 23 patients had at least one study-related serious adverse event. 16 patients stopped trastuzumab because of cardiac dysfunction.",0.6979,30.21,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication reports the same primary clinical construct, DFS, at the same 2-year timeframe and in the same TOP2A-defined population strata as the available registered primary outcome. Reporting 2-year OS additionally is consistent with a registered secondary outcome and does not constitute switching. The unavailable original registry version creates chronology uncertainty but provides no evidence of endpoint replacement or modification. The original/prospective registry version and any amendment history were unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; no major switch is inferred from the later wording alone. The registered secondary OS outcome was also reported in the publication.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,2-year disease-free survival (DFS) in TOP2A-amplified and TOP2A-non-amplified HER2+ patients treated with TC+H.,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Disease-free Survival (DFS) Rate at 2 Years in TOP2A-amplified and TOP2A-nonamplified HER2+ ESBC Patients Treated With TC+H."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""2-year DFS reported separately for TOP2A-amplified and TOP2A-non-amplified disease."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:55:11.952205+00:00
NCT01816594_28923573,Pathological Complete Response (pCR) Rate at the Time of Surgery - All Participants [Time Frame: After 6 weeks] | Pathological Complete Response (pCR) Rate at the Time of Surgery - PIK3CA Wild Type (WT) [Time Frame: After 6 weeks] | Pathological Complete Response (pCR) Rate at the Time of Surgery - PIK3CA Mutant (MT) [Time Frame: After 6 weeks],"Recruitment was suspended mainly due to liver toxicity after enrolment of 50 of the planned 256 patients. In each arm (buparlisib n = 25; placebo n = 25) 21 patients (84%) had wild type PIK3CA and 4 patients (16%) had mutant PIK3CA. Overall, pCR rate was similar between buparlisib and placebo arms (32.0% versus 40%; one-sided P = 0.811). A trend towards higher ORR (68.8% versus 33.3%; P = 0.053) and a significant decrease in Ki67 (75% versus 26.7%; P = 0.021) was observed in buparlisib versus placebo arm in the ER+ subgroup (Pinteraction = 0.03).",0.6298,37.02,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published principal endpoint is overall pCR, which is clinically equivalent to the registered all-participant pCR primary endpoint. The publication does not provide PIK3CA-stratified pCR results in the supplied text, but omission of registered primary subgroup results does not establish replacement or outcome switching. The reported ORR and Ki67 findings are additional analyses and do not change the pCR construct or hierarchy. Because the original registry version is unavailable, the conclusion is conservative. Registry change history was unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously and cannot establish whether the three pCR entries were the original prospective primary outcomes. No major switch is assigned on the basis of the current wording alone.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Overall pathological complete response (pCR) rate, comparing buparlisib versus placebo arms.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""pCR rate at the time of surgery after 6 weeks — all participants"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall pCR rate: 32.0% versus 40% between treatment arms"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""pCR rate at the time of surgery after 6 weeks — PIK3CA wild type"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No PIK3CA wild-type-specific pCR rate reported in the provided publication text"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""pCR rate at the time of surgery after 6 weeks — PIK3CA mutant"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No PIK3CA-mutant-specific pCR rate reported in the provided publication text"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:55:36.402788+00:00
NCT01160211_32822287,"Progression Free Survival (PFS) Events in Lapatinib + Trastuzumab + Aromatase Inhibitor (AI) vs. Trastuzumab + Aromatase Inhibitor (AI) [Time Frame: From date of randomization until date of progression or date of death from any cause, whichever comes first, assessed up approximately 5 years] | Median Kaplan Meier Estimates for PFS in Lapatinib + Trastuzumab + Aromatase Inhibitor (AI) vs. Trastuzumab + Aromatase Inhibitor (AI) [Time Frame: From date of randomization until date of progression or date of death from any cause, whichever comes first, assessed up approximately 5 years]","primary end point; superior PFS was observed with LAP plus TRAS plus AI versus TRAS plus AI (median PFS, 11 v 5",0.7038,29.62,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoints and published primary endpoint are the same PFS construct and treatment comparison. The publication reports both the PFS treatment effect and median PFS, which correspond to the registered event-based and median Kaplan-Meier PFS outcomes. The publication does not restate the full registered assessment timeframe, but this absence is not evidence of a change. ORR, CBR, and OS are registered secondary outcomes and were additionally reported, not switched into the primary position. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective primary outcome cannot be verified. The current registry entry was used cautiously and no switch was inferred from its wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS), with superior PFS for lapatinib plus trastuzumab plus aromatase inhibitor versus trastuzumab plus aromatase inhibitor; median PFS 11 versus 5.6 months.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""PFS events in lapatinib plus trastuzumab plus AI versus trastuzumab plus AI, from randomization until progression or death."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""PFS for lapatinib plus trastuzumab plus AI versus trastuzumab plus AI, reported with a hazard ratio and median PFS."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Median Kaplan-Meier estimates for PFS in lapatinib plus trastuzumab plus AI versus trastuzumab plus AI."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Median PFS of 11 versus 5.6 months for lapatinib plus trastuzumab plus AI versus trastuzumab plus AI."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:55:49.943645+00:00
NCT02040857_31250880,"2-Year Treatment Discontinuation Rate [Time Frame: Evaluate upon completion of palbociclib, up to 2 years of treatment completion.]","Overall, 162 patients initiated palbociclib; over half had stage III disease (52%) and most received prior chemotherapy (80%). A total of 102 patients (63%) completed 2 years of palbociclib; 50 patients discontinued early for protocol-related reasons (31%, 95% CI 24% to 39%, P = 0.001), and 10 discontinued due to protocol-unrelated reasons. The cumulative incidence of protocol-related discontinuation was 21% (95% CI 14% to 27%) at 12 months from start of treatment. Rates of palbociclib-related toxicity were congruent with the metastatic experience, and there were no cases of febrile neutropenia. Ninety-one patients (56%) required at least one dose reduction.",0.7589,24.11,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary outcome is the same treatment-discontinuation/completion construct and timeframe as the registered primary outcome. Reporting protocol-related discontinuations separately, along with protocol-unrelated discontinuations and 12-month cumulative incidence, does not replace the 2-year discontinuation endpoint. The listed toxicity outcomes are registered secondary outcomes rather than switched primary outcomes. No registry change history or original prospective version was available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; the absence of the original version prevents confirmation that the current wording was prospectively registered, but does not provide evidence of a switch.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"2-year treatment discontinuation/completion rate, reported through the proportion completing 2 years and discontinuations during treatment",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""2-Year Treatment Discontinuation Rate"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""2-year treatment discontinuation/completion rate; 102 of 162 patients completed 2 years, with treatment discontinuations also reported by reason"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:56:06.068315+00:00
NCT00811135_22467666,"Percentage of Participants With a Best Overall Response (BOR) of Confirmed Complete Response (CR) or Partial Response (PR) [Time Frame: Screening until disease progression (assessed at screening, every 6 weeks up to Week 36, thereafter every 9 weeks during treatment period, and then every 3 months during follow-up, up to approximately 4 years)]","primary endpoint, was 73% (95% confidence interval [CI], 62%-82%), comprising 7% complete and 66% partial responses",0.621,37.9,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are both overall/best response based on complete or partial responses. The publication does not restate all registry qualifiers, such as confirmation rules or assessment schedule, but no affirmative evidence demonstrates a different endpoint, timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry was used cautiously, and no major switch is inferred from the current wording alone.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Best overall response rate of complete or partial responses, reported as 73% (95% CI, 62%-82%), comprising 7% complete and 66% partial responses",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with a best overall response of confirmed complete response or partial response"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Best overall response rate comprising complete and partial responses"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:56:18.477952+00:00
NCT03250676_40830892,"Phase 1: Number of Participants With Dose-limiting Toxicities (DLTs) [Time Frame: Cycle 1 (Cycle length=28 days)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Objective Response Rate (ORR) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to the first date of documentation of progressive disease (PD) or death, whichever occurred first (up to 33 months)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Duration of Response (DoR) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to the first date of documentation of PD or death, whichever occurred first (up to 33 months)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Disease Control Rate (DCR) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to the first date of documentation of PD or death, whichever occurred first (up to 33 months)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to the first date of documentation of PD or death, whichever occurred first (up to 33 months)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Progression-free Survival (PFS) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to the first date of documentation of PD or death, whichever occurred first (up to 33 months)] | Phase 1 and Phase 2: Overall Survival (OS) [Time Frame: Phase 1 and Phase 2: From the first dose of study drug to date of death or last known alive (up to 63 months)]","primary endpoints were RP2D determination (phase 1) and objective response rate (ORR) (phase 2, investigator-assessed per RECIST v1",0.7298,27.02,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,moderate_switch,"The Phase 2 ORR endpoint is clinically concordant with the registered ORR. However, the publication explicitly presents RP2D determination as the Phase 1 primary endpoint, whereas the available registry entry identifies Phase 1 DLTs as the primary endpoint. RP2D determination is not the same clinical construct as the DLT endpoint. Because the original registry version and full publication results are unavailable, this supports a moderate, partly disclosed classification rather than a major undisclosed switch. Registry change history was unavailable, and the original prospective primary outcome cannot be established. The comparison therefore uses the current registry entry cautiously. The discrepancy between registered Phase 1 DLTs and published Phase 1 RP2D determination is explicit, but chronology and whether a prior amendment existed cannot be determined. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned. Endpoint 3: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 4: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 5: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 6: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 7: clinical-construct equivalence was not established.",medium,False,True,False,True,primary_demoted | prespecified_omitted,0.65,Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed,different,"RP2D determination (phase 1) and investigator-assessed Objective Response Rate (ORR) (phase 2, per RECIST v1).","The current registry identifies the Phase 1 primary endpoint as number of participants with DLTs, whereas the publication explicitly identifies RP2D determination as the Phase 1 primary endpoint.",False,Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned. Endpoint 3: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 4: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 5: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 6: clinical-construct equivalence was not established. Endpoint 7: clinical-construct equivalence was not established.,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Dose-limiting Toxicities (DLTs) (Phase 1; Cycle 1)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""RP2D determination (Phase 1)"", ""same_construct"": false, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": false, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The current registry identifies the Phase 1 primary endpoint as number of participants with DLTs, whereas the publication explicitly identifies RP2D determination as the Phase 1 primary endpoint.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate (ORR) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate (ORR) (Phase 2; investigator-assessed per RECIST v1)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Duration of Response (DoR) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No corresponding published primary endpoint identified"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Disease Control Rate (DCR) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No corresponding published primary endpoint identified"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No corresponding published primary endpoint identified"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free Survival (PFS) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No corresponding published primary endpoint identified"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall Survival (OS) (Phase 1 and Phase 2)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No corresponding published primary endpoint identified"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,moderate_switch,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:56:42.492242+00:00
NCT01853748_34077270,"Number of Participants of Clinically Relevant Toxicities (CRT) [Time Frame: 5 years after completion of study treatment or until death, whichever occurs first.] | 3-year Disease Free Survival (DFS) Rate of Trastuzumab Emtansine (TDM-1) [Time Frame: 3 years]","The analysis population includes all 497 patients who initiated protocol therapy (383 T-DM1 and 114 TH). CRTs were experienced by 46% of patients on T-DM1 and 47% of patients on TH (P = .83). The 3-year iDFS for T-DM1 was 97.8% (95% CI, 96.3 to 99.3), which rejected the null hypothesis (P < .0001). Serially collected patient-reported outcomes indicated that patients treated with T-DM1 had less neuropathy and alopecia and better work productivity compared with patients on TH.",0.5916,40.84,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"Both registry-listed primary constructs are reported in the publication. CRTs are reported as the proportion of patients experiencing clinically relevant toxicities, and the registered 3-year DFS endpoint is reported as 3-year iDFS, which is clinically equivalent absent affirmative evidence of a different definition. The publication does not restate every registered timeframe or operational detail, but that omission alone does not establish outcome modification or switching. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective primary endpoint(s) cannot be established independently. The current entry lists both CRTs and 3-year DFS as primary outcomes and is used cautiously; no major switch is inferred from the current wording.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Clinically relevant toxicities (CRTs) and 3-year invasive disease-free survival (iDFS) for T-DM1.,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants of Clinically Relevant Toxicities (CRT)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Proportion of patients experiencing clinically relevant toxicities (CRTs)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""3-year Disease-Free Survival (DFS) Rate of T-DM1"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""3-year invasive disease-free survival (iDFS) for T-DM1"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:56:58.545033+00:00
NCT03371017_38755096,Overall Survival (OS) in PD-L1-positive Population [Time Frame: Time from randomization to death (Up to 68 months)] | OS in Modified Intent-to-treat (mITT) Population [Time Frame: Time from randomization to death (Up to 68 months)],"Among 354 patients with rapidly relapsing PD-L1-positive aTNBC, 68% had a disease-free interval of <6 months and 73% received carboplatin/gemcitabine. The OS hazard ratio was 0.93 (95% confidence interval 0.73-1.20, P = 0.59; median 11.2 months with placebo versus 12.1 months with atezolizumab). mITT and subgroup results were consistent. Median PFS was 4 months across treatment arms and populations. ORRs were 28% with placebo versus 40% with atezolizumab. Adverse events (predominantly haematological) were similar between arms and as expected with atezolizumab plus carboplatin/gemcitabine or capecitabine following recent chemotherapy exposure.",0.6018,39.82,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary endpoint is OS, matching both registered primary OS endpoints in clinical construct and population. Reporting PFS, ORR, and adverse events does not replace the registered primary endpoint. The publication does not restate the full registered timeframe, but no different timeframe or definition is affirmatively reported. The original registry version is unavailable, so the current entry is interpreted cautiously. No registry change history was available. The original prospective primary outcome cannot be verified, so the current registry entry was used cautiously. The unavailable original version does not support assigning a switch.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.95,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Overall Survival (OS), including results in the PD-L1-positive population and mITT population",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall Survival (OS) in PD-L1-positive Population"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall Survival (OS) in PD-L1-positive patients"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall Survival (OS) in modified intent-to-treat (mITT) Population"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall Survival (OS) in the mITT population"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:57:12.072579+00:00
NCT01926886_32240454,Percentage of Participants With Adverse Events (AEs) and Serious Adverse Events (SAEs) [Time Frame: Up to 45 months],"Between November 2013 and December 2014, 23 centres enrolled 102 patients in the intent-to-treat population of which 101 patients entered the safety population. No new safety signals were detected with as expected, more mild administration site events with trastuzumab SC when compared to IV treatment. All patients agreed that they had benefit from at home administration to a large (18/81; 22%) or very large (63/81; 78%) extent. All HCPs (21/21) agreed that SC is the quickest method from start of preparation to finish of administration and that less resource use is needed.",0.4066,59.34,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication's principal reported clinical outcome is safety, which is clinically equivalent to the current registered primary endpoint of AEs and SAEs. Patient satisfaction and healthcare-professional assessments are registered secondary outcomes rather than replacement primary endpoints. The publication does not restate the full registered timeframe or operational safety definitions, but no affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. No registry change history is available, and the original prospective primary outcome cannot be verified. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously. The publication reports safety findings corresponding to the current registered primary; the patient and healthcare-professional assessments correspond to registered secondary outcomes.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.82,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Safety, including adverse events and serious adverse events during trastuzumab subcutaneous administration; the publication reports no new safety signals and mild administration-site events.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With Adverse Events (AEs) and Serious Adverse Events (SAEs) [Time Frame: Up to 45 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Safety/adverse events: no new safety signals were detected, with more mild administration-site events reported with trastuzumab SC than IV treatment."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:57:26.958259+00:00
NCT01605396_28681171,1. Progression-free Survival (PFS) According to Response Criteria in Solid Tumors Version 1.1 (RECIST 1.1) Based on Blinded Independent Central Review (BICR) [Time Frame: From Day 1 through last post-study efficacy follow-up (up to ~19 months)],Median PFS was 23.3 weeks for R/D/E versus 31.9 weeks for R/E (hazard ratio 1.18; 80% CI 0.81-1.72; P = 0.565). Grade 3-5 adverse events were reported in 67.5% of patients in the R/E arm and 59.0% in the R/D/E arm. Stomatitis (95.0 vs. 76.9%; P = 0.021) and pneumonitis (22.5 vs. 5.1%; P = 0.027) occurred more frequently in the R/E than the R/D/E arm; hyperglycemia (27.5 vs. 28.2%) occurred at a similar rate.,0.4827,51.73,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary are both progression-free survival. Reporting median PFS, a hazard ratio, and a different treatment comparison does not change the endpoint. The publication excerpt does not restate all registry qualifiers, such as RECIST version, BICR, or the registered timeframe, but it provides no affirmative evidence that these were changed. The registered primary remains reported; therefore, the pair is concordant. No registry change history is available, and the original prospective primary outcome cannot be verified. The current registry primary outcome was therefore used cautiously. The lack of historical information does not provide evidence of a switch.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.94,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS), reported as median PFS and compared between treatment arms",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free Survival (PFS) according to RECIST 1.1 based on BICR"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival (PFS), with median PFS and hazard ratio reported"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:57:38.694665+00:00
NCT01125566_26822398,"Progression-free Survival (PFS) [Time Frame: From randomization (07Sep2010) until disease progression, death or data cut-off (08Jun2013); Up to 34 months]","Between Aug 26, 2010, and April 26, 2013, we enrolled 508 patients: 339 assigned to the afatinib group and 169 assigned to the trastuzumab group. Recruitment was stopped on April 26, 2013, after a benefit-risk assessment by the independent data monitoring committee was unfavourable for the afatinib group. Patients on afatinib plus vinorelbine had to switch to trastuzumab plus vinorelbine, afatinib monotherapy, vinorelbine monotherapy, or receive treatment outside of the trial. Median follow-up was 9·3 months (IQR 3·7-16·0). Median progression-free survival was 5·5 months (95% CI 5·4-5·6) in the afatinib group and 5·6 months (5·3-7·3) in the trastuzumab group (hazard ratio 1·10 95% CI 0·86-1·41; p=0·43). The most common drug-related adverse events of grade 3 or higher were neutropenia (190 [56%] of 337 patients in the afatinib group vs 102 [60%] of 169 patients in the trastuzumab group), leucopenia (64 [19%] vs 34 [20%]), and diarrhoea (60 [18%] vs none).",0.4864,51.36,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary outcomes are both PFS, the same clinical construct. The publication reports PFS as the primary efficacy result; reporting medians and a hazard ratio does not change the endpoint. The publication does not restate all registry timeframe or definition details, but no affirmative evidence demonstrates a different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. The unreported registered secondary outcomes do not constitute primary outcome switching. No registry change history or original prospective primary outcome is available. The current registry entry lists PFS, which is used cautiously; no major switch is assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Median progression-free survival,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free Survival (PFS)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:57:50.184458+00:00
NCT03633331_38852379,Incidence of Adverse Events [Time Frame: 6 months],"Of the 90 participants (median age 74 years [70-87]) enrolled, 75.6% (95% confidence interval [CI], 65.4-84.0) had grade 3+ AEs by six months. The most frequent grade 3+ AEs were neutropenia (61%), fatigue (4%), and nausea (3%). Febrile neutropenia was uncommon (1.1%). Due to AEs, 36% had dose delays, 34% had dose reductions, 10% had early discontinuations, and 10% had hospitalizations. Compared to those aged 70-74 years, participants aged ≥75 years had higher rates of early discontinuations (5.9% vs 15.9%, a difference of 9.5% [95% CI 3.5%-22.5%]).",0.4825,51.75,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published outcomes address the same clinical construct—adverse-event incidence over six months. The publication specifies grade 3+ events, but the registry wording does not establish whether all grades or a specific severity threshold were intended. This is therefore treated as an unconfirmed definition difference rather than an outcome modification or switch. The registered primary outcome remains represented, and omitted secondary outcomes do not by themselves indicate primary outcome switching. Registry change history was unavailable. The current registry entry was used cautiously; no major switch was assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.83,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Incidence of grade 3+ adverse events by six months,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Incidence of Adverse Events [Time Frame: 6 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Incidence of grade 3+ adverse events by six months"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:58:06.927965+00:00
NCT00066703_24881463,Disease-free Survival [Time Frame: 5-year estimate reported at a median follow-up of 72 months],"After a median follow-up of 68 months, disease-free survival at 5 years was 91.1% in the exemestane-ovarian suppression group and 87.3% in the tamoxifen-ovarian suppression group (hazard ratio for disease recurrence, second invasive cancer, or death, 0.72; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.60 to 0.85; P<0.001). The rate of freedom from breast cancer at 5 years was 92.8% in the exemestane-ovarian suppression group, as compared with 88.8% in the tamoxifen-ovarian suppression group (hazard ratio for recurrence, 0.66; 95% CI, 0.55 to 0.80; P<0.001). With 194 deaths (4.1% of the patients), overall survival did not differ significantly between the two groups (hazard ratio for death in the exemestane-ovarian suppression group, 1.14; 95% CI, 0.86 to 1.51; P=0.37). Selected adverse events of grade 3 or 4 were reported for 30.6% of the patients in the exemestane-ovarian suppression group and 29.4% of those in the tamoxifen-ovarian suppression group, with profiles similar to those for postmenopausal women.",0.4866,51.34,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are both 5-year disease-free survival. The publication reports the registered endpoint directly, although the median follow-up differs and the publication provides additional definition detail. No affirmative evidence shows a changed endpoint, timeframe, population, or measurement method. The publication also reports other outcomes, including freedom from breast cancer and overall survival, but these do not replace the registered primary endpoint. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; its 5-year disease-free survival endpoint is concordant with the published primary endpoint. The difference between the registered and published median follow-up durations does not change the 5-year endpoint.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Disease-free survival at 5 years, after a median follow-up of 68 months",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Disease-free survival; 5-year estimate reported at a median follow-up of 72 months"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Disease-free survival at 5 years, defined by disease recurrence, second invasive cancer, or death"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:58:18.679836+00:00
NCT02988986_33860388,Ki67 Expression [Time Frame: Baseline to 6 weeks],"Twenty-eight women were enrolled on the trial, and 25 completed the entire study course. The combination of tamoxifen and TAK-228 resulted in a significant reduction in Ki-67 from 18.3 to 15.2% (p = 0.0023). The drug was also found to be safe and tolerable. While nausea and hyperglycemia were common side effects, these were manageable. The tumor size also significantly decreased with the treatment, with a median decrease of 0.75 cm (p < 0.0001). There were no pathologic complete responses.",0.4278,57.22,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary outcome is Ki-67 reduction, which is clinically equivalent to the current registered primary outcome of Ki67 Expression. The publication additionally reports tumor size, safety, and pCR, but these do not replace the Ki-67 primary endpoint. The six-week timeframe is not restated in the provided publication text, which is missing detail rather than evidence of a timeframe change. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry was used cautiously; the later wording alone is insufficient to assign an outcome switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.93,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Reduction in Ki-67 expression from baseline (18.3% to 15.2%; p = 0.0023),,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Ki67 Expression [Time Frame: Baseline to 6 weeks]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Reduction in Ki-67 expression from 18.3% to 15.2%"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:58:30.340945+00:00
NCT00934856_27052654,"Number of Participants With Dose Limiting Toxicity (DLT) - MBC and LABC Feasibility Population [Time Frame: Cycle 1 (up to 21 days)] | Percentage of Participants With Adverse Events (AEs) or Serious AEs (SAEs) - MBC and LABC Population [Time Frame: Baseline up to 28 days after last dose for MBC participants and for LABC participants who could not undergo surgery, and up to 6 weeks post-surgery for LABC participants who underwent surgery (maximum up to approximately 3 years)]","Administered with T-DM1 (3.6 mg/kg), the docetaxel MTD was 60 mg/m(2) in MBC. In LABC, the MTD was 100 mg/m(2) docetaxel in combination with T-DM1 (3.6 mg/kg), given with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF). Administered with T-DM1 (3.6 mg/kg) + pertuzumab (840 mg, cycle 1; 420 mg, subsequent cycles), the docetaxel MTD in LABC was 75 mg/m(2) with G-CSF support. Neutropenia was the most common grade 3-4 adverse event (AE; MBC, 72% and LABC, 29%). In total, 48% (12/25) of MBC patients and 47% (34/73) of LABC patients experienced AEs requiring dose modification. In MBC (median prior systemic agents = 5), the objective response rate was 80.0% (20/25; 95% confidence interval [CI] 59.3-93.2) and the median progression-free survival was 13.8 months (range, 1.6-33.5). The pathologic complete response (ypT0/is, ypN0) rate in LABC was 60.3% (44/73; 95% CI 48.1-71.5). Pharmacokinetic analyses indicated a low risk of drug-drug interaction between T-DM1 and docetaxel.",0.6117,38.83,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,moderate_switch,"The publication makes MTD, rather than the registered DLT count, the principal dose-finding outcome. MTD and DLT are related but clinically and operationally distinct endpoints. The other registered primary safety endpoint, AEs/SAEs, is represented by reported AE results. Because the original prospective registry version is unavailable and one registered primary remains reported, the evidence supports a moderate rather than major switch classification. Only the current registry entry is available, and the original prospective version cannot be established. The current entry was therefore used cautiously. A major switch is not assigned solely on the basis of the current registry wording. The publication explicitly presents MTD as the principal dose-finding result, whereas the current registry lists DLT and AE/SAE endpoints as primary outcomes. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.",medium,False,True,False,True,primary_replaced | prespecified_omitted,0.65,Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed,different,"Maximum tolerated dose (MTD) of docetaxel in combination with T-DM1, with or without pertuzumab, in MBC and LABC.",The registry specifies the primary endpoint as the number of participants with DLT. | The publication presents docetaxel MTD as the principal dose-finding result; MTD is a dose-level endpoint and is not the same endpoint as the number of participants experiencing DLT. | DLT results are not explicitly reported in the supplied publication text.,False,Endpoint 1: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Dose Limiting Toxicity (DLT) - MBC and LABC Feasibility Population [Cycle 1, up to 21 days]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Docetaxel maximum tolerated dose (MTD) in combination with T-DM1, with or without pertuzumab"", ""same_construct"": false, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": false, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The registry specifies the primary endpoint as the number of participants with DLT."", ""The publication presents docetaxel MTD as the principal dose-finding result; MTD is a dose-level endpoint and is not the same endpoint as the number of participants experiencing DLT."", ""DLT results are not explicitly reported in the supplied publication text.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With AEs or SAEs - MBC and LABC Population [baseline through post-treatment follow-up]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Adverse events, including grade 3-4 AEs and AEs requiring dose modification, reported for MBC and LABC"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,moderate_switch,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:58:55.426386+00:00
NCT02860000_36892847,Tumor Response Rate Defined as 100% Times the Number of Patients Who Meet the Criteria for Complete Response (CR) or Partial Response (PR) Using RECIST Criteria Version 1.1 [Time Frame: Up to 4.5 years],"All 91 evaluable patients (mean [SD] age, 58.5 [11.3] years; 1 American Indian/Alaskan Native [1.1%], 2 Asian [2.2%], 6 Black/African American [6.6%], 5 Hispanic [5.5%], and 79 [86.8%] White individuals; arm 1, 46 [50.5%]; arm 2, 45 [49.5%]) had received prior treatment with CDK 4/6i. The ORR was 19.6%; (90% CI, 10.6%-31.7%) for arm 1 and 20.0% (90% CI, 10.9%-32.3%) for arm 2. In arm 1, the 24-week clinical benefit rate and median progression-free survival time were 41.3% (90% CI, 29.0%-54.5%) and 5.6 months (95% CI, 3.9-10.0), respectively, and in arm 2 they were 28.9% (90% CI, 18.0%-42.0%) and 5.4 months (95% CI, 3.9-7.8), respectively. The most common grade 3 or higher adverse events attributed to alisertib were neutropenia (41.8%) and anemia (13.2%). Reasons for discontinuing treatment were disease progression (arm 1, 38 [82.6%]; arm 2, 31 [68.9%]) and toxic effects or refusal (arm 1, 5 [10.9%]; arm 2, 12 [26.7%]).",0.4598,54.02,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are both response rate based on complete or partial response. Reporting ORR by treatment arm does not change the endpoint. The publication does not restate all registered timeframe or RECIST qualifiers in the supplied text, but this absence is not evidence of modification. Missing registered secondary outcomes do not constitute primary outcome switching. No registry change history or original prospective version was available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; its wording alone does not support an outcome switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.96,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Overall response rate (ORR), reported by treatment arm.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Tumor Response Rate defined by complete response or partial response using RECIST version 1.1 [up to 4.5 years]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall response rate (ORR), reported by treatment arm"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:59:07.436210+00:00
NCT01923298_32328775,"Changes in Serum Estradiol [Time Frame: Baseline, 16 weeks]","The median age for all patients was 55 years, and the majority of them were treated with anastrozole. There was no significant difference between baseline and week 16 estradiol levels (p = 0.81). In addition, patients in the prospective group reported subjective improvement in their vaginal dryness symptoms questionnaires.",0.6095,39.05,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary outcome measures serum estradiol change from baseline to week 16, matching the current registered primary endpoint. The report also mentions subjective improvement in vaginal dryness symptoms, but this does not replace or modify the registered estradiol endpoint and is not explicitly identified as a prespecified primary outcome. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry specifies change in serum estradiol from baseline to 16 weeks, which corresponds to the published primary outcome. No major switch is assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.96,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Change in serum estradiol levels from baseline to week 16,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Changes in serum estradiol"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Change in serum estradiol levels from baseline to week 16"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:59:15.698181+00:00
NCT00033514_19299235,The Objective Response Rate as Defined as Stable Disease or the Rate of Complete and Partial Responses Determined on Two Consecutive Occasions Greater Than or Equal to 4 Weeks Apart. [Time Frame: 5 years] | Recommended Dose for Phase II [Time Frame: treatment period],"The combination was well tolerated among the 16 patients enrolled in phase I, and the recommended phase II dose of erlotinib was initially set at 150 mg. After an interim review of the first 8 patients in phase II revealed a higher incidence of rash and diarrhea than expected from the phase I experience, the protocol was amended to treat new phase II patients at erlotinib 100 mg, with the opportunity to escalate to 150 mg after 3 weeks, based on individual patient tolerability. As a result of advances in other therapies aimed at HER2+ breast cancer, phase II closed before meeting its accrual goal. Among the 12 evaluable chemotherapy- and trastuzumab-naive patients treated at the recommended phase II dose level, there were 4 partial responses, and the time to progression was 9.03 months (95% CI, 1.2-undetermined). No pharmacokinetic interaction between the 2 agents was observed.",0.5959,40.41,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"Both current registered primary outcomes are represented in the publication: the recommended phase II dose is reported, and response activity is reported through partial responses from which the response outcome is represented. The publication's additional report of time to progression does not replace the registered primary outcomes. Missing confirmation details, assessment schedule, and timeframe in the extracted publication text are not affirmative evidence of endpoint modification. No registry change history was available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously and cannot establish the original prospective wording or timing of any amendment. The publication explicitly discloses a protocol amendment affecting dose administration, not replacement of the registered primary endpoints.",high,True,True,True,False,,0.84,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Recommended phase II dose of erlotinib and preliminary antitumor activity, including 4 partial responses among 12 evaluable patients at the recommended phase II dose; time to progression was also reported.",The publication explicitly states that the protocol was amended after an interim safety review and that new phase II patients received erlotinib 100 mg with potential escalation to 150 mg based on tolerability.,False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate, defined as stable disease or the rate of complete and partial responses determined on two consecutive occasions at least 4 weeks apart [5 years]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Antitumor activity measured by 4 partial responses among 12 evaluable patients treated at the recommended phase II dose"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Recommended Dose for Phase II [treatment period]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Recommended phase II dose of erlotinib, initially 150 mg and subsequently individualized between 100 mg and 150 mg after a protocol amendment"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": true, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The publication explicitly states that the protocol was amended after an interim safety review and that new phase II patients received erlotinib 100 mg with potential escalation to 150 mg based on tolerability.""], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:59:35.937874+00:00
NCT01565083_27955684,"Percentage of Participants With Best Overall Response (BOR) as Assessed by Investigator According to Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors Version 1.1 (RECIST v1.1) [Time Frame: Baseline, every 3 cycles up to 36 months, and every 6 cycles thereafter if progression free after 36 months, 28 days after end of treatment, every 3 months thereafter (maximum up to approximately 3.5 years)]","Cohort 1 enrolled 106 patients. Investigator-assessed ORR was 74.2% (95% CI 63.8-82.9) in intent-to-treat patients with measurable disease (89/106 [84.0%]). Median PFS was 14.3 months (95% CI 11.2-17.5) in the intent-to-treat population. Treatment was reasonably well tolerated, with no unexpected toxicities. Diarrhea (61/106 patients [57.5%]) and neutropenia (54/106 [50.9%]) were the most common adverse events (AEs); neutropenia (33/106 [31.1%]) and leukopenia (14/106 [13.2%]) were the most common grade ≥3 AEs. Serious AEs were reported in 32/106 (30.2%) patients. AEs led to study drug discontinuation in 36/106 patients (34.0%). Eighteen of 106 patients (17.0%) had AEs suggestive of congestive heart failure; however, there were no confirmed cases.",0.5868,41.32,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint both assess tumor response using investigator-assessed RECIST criteria. The publication uses the clinically equivalent ORR summary rather than restating the registry's BOR wording. Median PFS is a reported secondary outcome and does not replace the response endpoint. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method, and the absence of the original registry version prevents a stronger chronology-based inference. No registry change history is available. The current registry wording was used cautiously and cannot be treated as definitive evidence of the original prospectively registered endpoint or of a switch. The published ORR is clinically consistent with investigator-assessed RECIST best response.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.82,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Investigator-assessed objective response rate (ORR), 74.2% in the intent-to-treat patients with measurable disease.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With Best Overall Response (BOR) as Assessed by Investigator According to RECIST v1.1"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Investigator-assessed objective response rate (ORR)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T20:59:50.279238+00:00
NCT03176238_33728524,Summary of Number of Participants With Treatment Emergent Adverse Events (TEAE) - All Grades [Time Frame: Baseline up to approximately 43 weeks for Asian countires and 32 weeks for Non-Asian countries including a 30 day post treatment follow up period],"A total of 235 patients received ≥ 1 dose of study medication. At the end of the study, all patients ceased the treatment. Disease progression (66.0%) was the primary reason of discontinuation. The most common AEs (≥ 20%) were stomatitis, decreased appetite, hyperglycemia, rash, aspartate aminotransferase increased, anemia, alanine aminotransferase increased, cough, and fatigue. No new safety concerns were identified in the current study. Median progression-free survival (PFS) in the Asian subset was similar to that of the overall population (9.3 months in both groups). Confirmed overall response rate (ORR) was achieved for 19.6% of the patients. Efficacy of EVE + EXE across subgroups (prior CT, line of treatment, and presence of visceral metastases) was maintained.",0.5542,44.58,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint is an all-grade TEAE safety outcome. The publication reports safety and adverse-event findings as part of its principal results, including common AEs and no new safety concerns. Although the publication does not restate every registry qualifier, timeframe, or endpoint label, it provides no affirmative evidence of a different safety endpoint. PFS and confirmed ORR are reported as efficacy outcomes and do not replace the registered safety primary. Missing secondary outcomes do not constitute primary outcome switching. No registry change history or original prospective version was available. The current registry entry was used cautiously; no major switch was inferred from the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.92,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Safety, including treatment-emergent adverse events (AEs) and common all-grade AEs",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Summary of Number of Participants With Treatment Emergent Adverse Events (TEAE) - All Grades"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Safety and adverse events, including the most common AEs and absence of new safety concerns"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:00:01.455414+00:00
NCT02413320_33208340,Number of Participants With Pathological Complete Response [Time Frame: 20 weeks],"One hundred patients were randomized; arm A (n = 48) or arm B (n = 52). pCR was 54% [95% confidence interval (CI), 40%-69%] in arm A and 54% (95% CI, 40%-68%) in arm B. RCB 0+I rate was 67% in both arms. Median sTIL density was numerically higher in those with pCR compared with those with residual disease (20% vs. 5%; P = 0.25). At median follow-up of 38 months, EFS and OS were similar in the two arms. Grade 3/4 adverse events were more common in arm A compared with arm B, with the most notable differences in neutropenia (60% vs. 8%; P < 0.001) and febrile neutropenia (19% vs. 0%; P < 0.001). There was one treatment-related death (arm A) due to acute leukemia. Mean treatment cost was lower for arm B compared with arm A (P = 0.02).",0.5084,49.16,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The published primary endpoint is pCR, which is the same clinical construct as the currently registered pathological complete response endpoint. The publication does not restate the registered 20-week timeframe, but no different timeframe is affirmatively reported; this is missing detail rather than evidence of modification. Additional outcomes are reported, but the registered primary endpoint remains intact. The original prospective registry version and change history are unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore treated cautiously and was not used to infer a major switch. The current registered primary, pathological complete response at 20 weeks, is clinically equivalent to the published pCR endpoint.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Pathological complete response (pCR), reported as 54% in arm A and 54% in arm B",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Pathological Complete Response at 20 weeks"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Pathological complete response (pCR)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:00:15.014486+00:00
NCT00976989_23704196,Safety: Percentage of Participants With Symptomatic Cardiac Events as Assessed by the Investigator [Time Frame: From baseline up to approximately 3.5 years] | Safety: Percentage of Participants With Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction (LVEF) Decline During Pre-operative (Neoadjuvant) Period [Time Frame: From baseline up to approximately 18 weeks],"Two hundred twenty-five patients were randomized. During neoadjuvant treatment, two patients (2.7%; Arm B) experienced symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction (LVSD) and 11 patients (Arm A: 4 [5.6%]; Arm B: 4 [5.3%]; Arm C: 3 [3.9%]) had declines in left ventricular ejection fraction of ≥10% points from baseline to <50%. Diarrhea was the most common adverse event. pCR (ypT0/is) was reported for 61.6% (Arm A), 57.3% (Arm B), and 66.2% (Arm C) of patients.",0.6345,36.55,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication's primary safety findings report the same cardiac safety constructs as the available registered primary outcomes. The LVEF endpoint is directly concordant, including the neoadjuvant timeframe and threshold. Symptomatic LVSD is clinically equivalent to the registered symptomatic cardiac-event construct; the excerpt does not establish a different definition or a definitive replacement of the longer registered follow-up timeframe. Any apparent difference is therefore limited to details not restated in the supplied publication text. Registry history was unavailable, so the current registry entry was used cautiously. The original prospective primary outcome(s) cannot be confirmed. The available current primary outcomes correspond to the cardiac safety outcomes reported in the publication; no major switch is assigned based on the later registry wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Cardiac safety during neoadjuvant treatment, comprising symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction and declines in LVEF of at least 10 percentage points from baseline to below 50%.",,False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with symptomatic cardiac events as assessed by the investigator, from baseline up to approximately 3.5 years."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction during neoadjuvant treatment."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with LVEF decline during the pre-operative (neoadjuvant) period, from baseline up to approximately 18 weeks."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Decline in LVEF of at least 10 percentage points from baseline to below 50% during neoadjuvant treatment."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:00:27.913919+00:00
NCT00829166_23020162,"Percentage of Participants With PD or Death as Assessed by an Independent Review Committee (IRC) [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 14 Jan 2012 (up to 2 years, 11 months)] | Progression-free Survival (PFS) as Assessed by an IRC (Co-primary Endpoint) [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 14 Jan 2012 (up to 2 years, 11 months)] | Percentage of Participants Who Died: Second Interim Analysis [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 31 Jul 2012 (up to 3 years, 5 months)] | Overall Survival: Second Interim Analysis (Co-primary Endpoint) [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 31 Jul 2012 (up to 3 years, 5 months)] | Percentage of Participants Who Died: Final Analysis [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 31 Dec 2014 (up to 5 years, 11 months)] | Overall Survival: Final Analysis [Time Frame: From the date of randomization through the data cut-off date of 31 Dec 2014 (up to 5 years, 11 months)] | Percentage of Participants Who Were Alive at Year 1 [Time Frame: Year 1] | Percentage of Participants Who Were Alive at Year 2 [Time Frame: Year 2]","Among 991 randomly assigned patients, median progression-free survival as assessed by independent review was 9.6 months with T-DM1 versus 6.4 months with lapatinib plus capecitabine (hazard ratio for progression or death from any cause, 0.65; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.55 to 0.77; P<0.001), and median overall survival at the second interim analysis crossed the stopping boundary for efficacy (30.9 months vs. 25.1 months; hazard ratio for death from any cause, 0.68; 95% CI, 0.55 to 0.85; P<0.001). The objective response rate was higher with T-DM1 (43.6%, vs. 30.8% with lapatinib plus capecitabine; P<0.001); results for all additional secondary end points favored T-DM1. Rates of grade 3 or 4 adverse events were higher with lapatinib plus capecitabine than with T-DM1 (57% vs. 41%). The incidences of thrombocytopenia and increased serum aminotransferase levels were higher with T-DM1, whereas the incidences of diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia were higher with lapatinib plus capecitabine.",0.4752,52.48,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,moderate_switch,"The publication reports the principal registered clinical constructs: IRC-assessed PFS and second-interim overall survival. Reporting medians and hazard ratios rather than event percentages does not change the endpoint. The publication's statement that overall survival was assessed at the second interim analysis directly corresponds to the registered co-primary endpoint. Later final-analysis and fixed-year survival measures are not reported here, but they are not evidence that the registered PFS or second-interim OS endpoints were replaced or demoted. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective primary endpoint cannot be confirmed. The current entry must be interpreted cautiously. It includes IRC-assessed PFS and second-interim overall survival as co-primary endpoints, both of which are reported in the publication. The final-analysis and fixed-year survival measures are not reported in this publication, but their absence does not establish outcome switching. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 5: evidence recovered from top-level fields, not the structured per-endpoint array — flagged for review. Endpoint 5: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned. Endpoint 6: evidence recovered from top-level fields, not the structured per-endpoint array — flagged for review. Endpoint 6: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.",medium,False,True,False,False,primary_replaced | prespecified_omitted,0.65,Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed,different,IRC-assessed progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis.,"The current registry lists IRC-assessed progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis as co-primary endpoints, along with related event-percentage and later follow-up measures. | The publication reports IRC-assessed median progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis, including hazard ratios for progression or death and death, respectively. | The current registry lists IRC-assessed progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis as co-primary endpoints, along with related event-percentage and later follow-up measures. | The publication reports IRC-assessed median progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis, including hazard ratios for progression or death and death, respectively.",False,"Endpoint 5: evidence recovered from top-level fields, not the structured per-endpoint array — flagged for review. Endpoint 5: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned. Endpoint 6: evidence recovered from top-level fields, not the structured per-endpoint array — flagged for review. Endpoint 6: omission is evidenced but disclosure is unclear; major switch was not assigned.","[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with progression or death as assessed by an independent review committee (IRC)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""IRC-assessed progression-free survival, reported as median PFS and hazard ratio for progression or death"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival as assessed by an IRC"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""IRC-assessed progression-free survival"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants who died: second interim analysis"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival at the second interim analysis, reported as median overall survival and hazard ratio for death"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall survival: second interim analysis"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival at the second interim analysis"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants who died: final analysis"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival reported at the second interim analysis; final-analysis mortality percentage is not reported in this publication"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": false, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The current registry lists IRC-assessed progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis as co-primary endpoints, along with related event-percentage and later follow-up measures."", ""The publication reports IRC-assessed median progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis, including hazard ratios for progression or death and death, respectively.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall survival: final analysis"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival reported at the second interim analysis; final overall survival analysis is not reported in this publication"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": false, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The current registry lists IRC-assessed progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis as co-primary endpoints, along with related event-percentage and later follow-up measures."", ""The publication reports IRC-assessed median progression-free survival and overall survival at the second interim analysis, including hazard ratios for progression or death and death, respectively.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — moderate / partly disclosed""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants who were alive at Year 1"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival, reported as median overall survival and hazard ratio"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants who were alive at Year 2"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall survival, reported as median overall survival and hazard ratio"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,moderate_switch,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:00:59.405660+00:00
NCT02187991_33877311,"Time to Disease Progression - Tumor Response Based on RECIST 1.1 Criteria [Time Frame: until disease progression (assessed by RECIST 1.1), unacceptable toxicity, death, or discontinuation from study for any other reason, up to 3 years from date of patient registration]","A total of 174 patients were randomized, including with 86 randomized to paclitaxel and 88 patients randomized to paclitaxel plus alisertib, and 169 patients received study treatment. The final cohort included 139 patients with a median (interquartile range [IQR]) age of 62 (27-84) years with ER-positive and ERBB2-negative MBC, with 70 randomized to paclitaxel and 69 randomized to paclitaxel plus alisertib. The TNBC cohort closed with only 35 patients enrolled due to slow accrual and were not included in efficacy analyses. The median (IQR) follow-up was 22 (10.6-25.1) months, and median (IQR) PFS was 10.2 (3.8-15.7) months with paclitaxel plus alisertib vs 7.1 (3.8-10.6) months with paclitaxel alone (HR, 0.56; 95% CI, 0.37-0.84; P = .005). Median (IQR) OS was 26.3 (12.4-37.2) months for patients who received paclitaxel plus alisertib vs 25.1 (11.0-31.4) months for paclitaxel alone (HR, 0.89; 95% CI, 0.58-1.38; P = .61). Grade 3 or 4 adverse events occurred in 56 patients (84.8%) receiving paclitaxel plus alisertib vs 34 patients (48.6%) receiving paclitaxel alone. The main grade 3 or 4 adverse events with paclitaxel plus alisertib vs paclitaxel alone were neutropenia (50 patients [59.5%] vs 14 patients [16.4%]), anemia (8 patients [9.5%] vs 1 patient [1.2%]), diarrhea (9 patients [10.7%] vs 0 patients), and stomatitis or oral mucositis (13 patients [15.5%] vs 0 patients). One patient receiving paclitaxel plus alisertib died of sepsis.",0.443,55.7,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,minor_modification,"The registered and published outcomes are both progression-based time-to-event endpoints, so the underlying clinical construct is recognizably related. However, the publication explicitly excludes the TNBC cohort from efficacy analyses and presents efficacy in the ER-positive, ERBB2-negative cohort, indicating a disclosed change in the analysis population. This supports an outcome modification rather than an undisclosed outcome switch. The absence of the original registry version prevents confirmation of the original prospective wording. No registry change history was available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously and cannot be confirmed as the original prospective version.",high,False,True,True,False,definition_or_timeframe_changed,0.8,Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population),modified,"Progression-free survival (PFS), reported as median PFS and hazard ratio for paclitaxel plus alisertib versus paclitaxel","The publication states that the TNBC cohort enrolled 35 patients but was not included in efficacy analyses. | Published efficacy results were based on the ER-positive, ERBB2-negative metastatic breast cancer cohort.",False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Time to Disease Progression based on RECIST 1.1"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival (PFS)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": false, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": true, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The publication states that the TNBC cohort enrolled 35 patients but was not included in efficacy analyses."", ""Published efficacy results were based on the ER-positive, ERBB2-negative metastatic breast cancer cohort.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,minor_modification,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:01:23.933001+00:00
NCT03262935_39442070,Progression Free Survival [Time Frame: baseline until primary analysis data cut-off date of 31March2021],"In total, 437 patients were randomly assigned 2:1 to T-Duo (n = 291) or PC (n = 146). The median age was 56.0 years (range, 24-86); most patients (93.6%) had metastatic disease. The median time from diagnosis of metastatic disease to trial entry was 3.5 years; the median number of prior HER2-targeted therapies in metastatic setting was three. The median PFS was 7.0 months (95% CI, 5.4 to 7.2) with T-Duo versus 4.9 months (95% CI, 4.0 to 5.5; hazard ratio [HR], 0.64 [95% CI, 0.49 to 0.84]; P = .002) with PC. PFS benefit was maintained across most predefined subgroups. The median overall survival (first analysis) was 20.4 (T-Duo) versus 16.3 months (PC; HR, 0.83 [95% CI, 0.62 to 1.09]; P = .153). Objective response rate was 27.8% (T-Duo) versus 29.5% (PC); other efficacy end points-clinical benefit rate, duration of response, and reduction in target lesion measurement-tended to favor T-Duo. Grade ≥3 treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 52.8% (T-Duo) versus 48.2% (PC).",0.4901,50.99,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary are both progression-free survival. Reporting median PFS and a hazard ratio does not change the endpoint. The publication also reports secondary and other efficacy outcomes, but PFS remains the principal reported outcome. The original registry version is unavailable, so the current registry entry is interpreted cautiously. The original/prospective registry version is unavailable and no registry change history can be retrieved. The current registry entry lists progression-free survival as the primary outcome and is therefore used cautiously. Because the publication also reports progression-free survival as its primary efficacy outcome, there is no basis for assigning a switch from the current wording.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS), reported as median PFS and treatment-group hazard ratio.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression Free Survival"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival, including median PFS and hazard ratio comparing T-Duo versus PC"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:01:38.119925+00:00
NCT02520063_36735117,Number of Participants Who Experienced Dose-limiting Toxicities [Time Frame: 4 weeks],"Fifteen patients enrolled. The recommended phase 2 dose of everolimus in combination with letrozole and carotuximab was 10 mg PO daily. The most frequent adverse events were headache (67%), fatigue (47%), facial flushing and swelling (47%), gingival hemorrhage (40%), epistaxis (33%), nausea and vomiting (27%). Headache constituted a dose-limiting toxicity. At least two signs of mucocutaneous telangiectasia developed in 92% of patients. Carotuximab accumulated in the extravascular space and accelerated the biodistribution and clearance of everolimus. All patients had residual disease. Gene expression analyses were consistent with downregulation of genes involved in proliferation and DNA repair. Among 6 patients with luminal B breast cancer, 5 converted to luminal A after one cycle of therapy.",0.4191,58.09,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered current primary endpoint is dose-limiting toxicity, and the publication reports dose-limiting toxicity and tolerability as the basis for determining the recommended phase 2 dose. The additional RP2D statement is a dose-finding result derived from the safety endpoint rather than a replacement clinical outcome. The publication does not restate the registered 4-week timeframe or provide all endpoint-definition details in the supplied text, but absence of those details is not evidence of a modification. Only the current registry entry is available; the original prospective primary outcome and any amendments cannot be established. The current registered primary outcome was therefore used cautiously, and no switch was assigned based on the unavailable registry history.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Dose-limiting toxicity/tolerability, with determination of the recommended phase 2 dose",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants Who Experienced Dose-limiting Toxicities"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Dose-limiting toxicity/tolerability, including identification of the recommended phase 2 dose"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:01:54.628821+00:00
NCT02648477_36077811,Number of Participants With Overall Response [Time Frame: Up to 3 years],Primary endpoints were safety and overall response rate,0.4619,53.81,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,additional_outcome,"The published overall response rate is clinically equivalent to the registered number of participants with overall response, and no explicit change in timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method is reported. Safety is additionally listed as a published primary endpoint, but the unavailable original registry version prevents determining whether it was prospectively registered. The apparent timeframe discrepancy is due only to non-restatement in the provided publication text. Only the current registry entry is available. The original/prospective primary outcome cannot be established, so the current overall-response outcome was used cautiously and no major switch was assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,True,True,False,unregistered_added_disclosed,0.82,Additional outcome (disclosed exploratory),clinically equivalent,Safety and overall response rate,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Overall Response [Time Frame: Up to 3 years]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall response rate"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": true, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Additional outcome (disclosed exploratory)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,additional_outcome,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:02:06.428640+00:00
NCT03167619_38236575,"Efficacy of Olaparib Alone as Assessed by PFS (Progression-free Survival) Reported as Events Per Month [Time Frame: From date of randomization until the date of documented progression or date of death, whichever came first, approximately 1 year.] | Efficacy of Olaparib in Combination With Durvalumab as Assessed by PFS (Progression-free Survival) [Time Frame: From date of randomization until the date of documented progression or date of death, whichever came first, approximately 1 year.]","45 patients were randomized (23 to olaparib alone, 22 to the combination; 3 with estrogen/progesterone receptor expression 1%-10%). At 9.8 months' median follow-up, median PFS from randomization was 4.0 [95% confidence interval (CI), 2.6-6.1] months with olaparib and 6.1 (95% CI, 3.7-10.1) months with the combination, both significantly longer than the historical control (P = 0.0023 and P < 0.0001, respectively). Clinical benefit rates (SD ≥24 weeks or CR/PR) were 44% (95% CI, 23%-66%) and 36% (95% CI, 17%-59%) in the monotherapy and combination arms, respectively. Sustained clinical benefit was seen irrespective of germline BRCA mutation or PD-L1 status, but tended to be associated with CR/PR to prior platinum, particularly in the olaparib-alone arm. No new safety signals were reported.",0.8043,19.57,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary outcomes are both PFS for the randomized treatment arms. Reporting median PFS and using a historical-control comparison does not change the endpoint construct. The publication also reports clinical benefit rates, which are registered secondary outcomes, but this does not constitute outcome switching. Details not restated in the extracted publication text are not treated as evidence of modification. No registry change history is available and the original prospective registry version cannot be established. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; no major switch is inferred from the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.95,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS) from randomization, reported as median PFS by treatment arm and compared with a historical control.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""PFS for olaparib alone, from randomization until documented progression or death, approximately 1 year."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Median PFS from randomization with olaparib alone."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""PFS for olaparib plus durvalumab, from randomization until documented progression or death, approximately 1 year."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Median PFS from randomization with olaparib plus durvalumab."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:02:20.626753+00:00
NCT00871858_26171933,Objective Response Rate (ORR) Determined by Clinical Palpation [Time Frame: 6 months],"A total of 108 patients were evaluable for efficacy and 118 for toxicity. The objective response rate determined by clinical palpation was 58.9% (95% CI=45.0-71.9) in the anastrozole arm and 53.8% (95% CI=39.5-67.8) in the fulvestrant arm. The breast-conserving surgery rate was 58.9% (95% CI=45.0-71.9) in the anastrozole arm and 50.0% (95% CI=35.8-64.2) in the fulvestrant arm. Pathological responses >50% occurred in 24 patients (42.9%) in the anastrozole arm and 13 (25.0%) in the fulvestrant arm. The Ki-67 score fell after treatment but there was no significant difference between the reduction in the two arms (anastrozole 16.7% (95% CI=13.3-21.0) before, 3.2% (95% CI=1.9-5.5) after, n=43; fulvestrant 17.1% (95%CI=13.1-22.5) before, 3.2% (95% CI=1.8-5.7) after, n=38) or between the reduction in Ki-67 in clinical responders and non-responders. Genomic analysis appeared to show a reduction of clonal diversity following treatment with selection of some clones with simpler copy number profiles.",0.4566,54.34,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary endpoint and the published primary endpoint are the same clinical construct and use the same clinical palpation measurement method. The publication does not restate the registered 6-month timeframe, but no different timeframe is affirmatively reported; this is missing detail only. The publication also reports registered secondary outcomes and other analyses, none of which demonstrates replacement or demotion of the registered primary endpoint. The original prospective registry version and change history were unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously, and no outcome switch was inferred from the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Objective response rate determined by clinical palpation,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate (ORR) determined by clinical palpation [time frame: 6 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Objective response rate determined by clinical palpation, reported by treatment arm"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": true, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:02:34.305551+00:00
NCT01289353_28405497,Number of Patients Who Developed Grade 2-3 Acute Radiation Dermatitis Within 60 Days Post-RT [Time Frame: 60 days post-RT],"Purpose: To determine feasibility and explore the clinical efficacy of concurrent radiotherapy and carboplatin as adjuvant treatment of triple negative breast cancer (TNBC). Patients and Methods: Women with Stage I-II TNBC were treated after surgery in a phase I-II prospective trial [NCT01289353]. Weekly carboplatin (AUC = 2.0) was delivered for 6 weeks. Concurrent radiotherapy was delivered in the prone position during weeks 2-4, for a total dose of 40.5 Gy in 15 fractions to the breast, and 46.5 Gy in 17 fractions to the tumor bed. Adverse events (AE) were assessed weekly during treatment, once at 45-60 d, and every 6 mo thereafter, using the Common Terminology Criteria for AE (CTCAE) v3.0. Results: A total of 39 patients accrued and 36 received treatment. Eight patients (22%, exact 95% CI: 10%, 39%) developed grade 2 or greater acute radiation dermatitis. Overall, grade 2 AE were seen in nine and grade 3 in two patients. Twenty-three patients (64%) received additional adjuvant chemotherapy. With a median follow-up of 48 mo, 34/36 (94%) are alive and disease free. One patient died of pulmonary failure with possible but unproven breast cancer recurrence, and one patient died of pelvic malignancy. One patient recurred locally and is alive and disease free after surgical management. Brisk lymphocytic infiltrate was present pre-treatment in 39% of 18 patients with evaluable tumor. Conclusions: Adjuvant concurrent carboplatin and prone accelerated radiotherapy is a well-tolerated and promising treatment of early stage TNBC. The observed 3% compares favorably with the expected 30% recurrence rate within 1-4 y from treatment, warranting further studies.",0.4686,53.14,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,minor_modification,"The registered and published primary endpoints address the same clinical construct and the same acute post-radiotherapy period. However, the publication explicitly broadens the severity criterion from grade 2-3 to grade 2 or greater. This is a definition modification rather than a replacement of the registered endpoint, so it does not constitute a moderate or major outcome switch. Registry change history was unavailable, so the current registry entry cannot be confirmed as the original prospective version. The current primary endpoint was used cautiously; no major switch is assigned solely on the basis of the unavailable registry history.",high,False,True,False,False,definition_or_timeframe_changed,0.93,Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population),modified,"Number of patients developing grade 2 or greater acute radiation dermatitis, assessed 45-60 days after radiotherapy","The registry specifies grade 2-3 acute radiation dermatitis. | The publication reports grade 2 or greater acute radiation dermatitis, which does not restrict the endpoint to grades 2-3.",False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Patients Who Developed Grade 2-3 Acute Radiation Dermatitis Within 60 Days Post-RT"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Patients developing grade 2 or greater acute radiation dermatitis, assessed 45-60 days after radiotherapy"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": false, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The registry specifies grade 2-3 acute radiation dermatitis."", ""The publication reports grade 2 or greater acute radiation dermatitis, which does not restrict the endpoint to grades 2-3.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,minor_modification,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:02:54.325350+00:00
NCT01491737_30106636,Progression-Free Survival (PFS) [Time Frame: Median [full range] of follow-up time on study for: Primary Analysis: 31.7 [0.0-44.3] months vs. 30.4 [0.0-45.8] months in Arm A vs. Arm B; Final Analysis: 73.20 [0.03-88.34] months vs. 71.06 [0.03-88.97] months in Arm A vs. Arm B],"One hundred twenty-nine patients were randomly assigned per arm (February 2012 to October 2014; intent-to-treat populations); 75 in one arm and 71 in the other were chosen to receive induction chemotherapy. Stratified median PFS was 18.89 months (95% CI, 14.09 to 27.66 months) in the pertuzumab plus trastuzumab arm and 15.80 months (95% CI, 11.04 to 18.56 months) in the trastuzumab arm (stratified hazard ratio, 0.65; 95% CI, 0.48 to 0.89; P = .0070). Serious adverse events (AEs) were reported for 42 (33.1%) of 127 and 24 (19.4%) of 124 patients in the safety populations of the pertuzumab plus trastuzumab and trastuzumab arms, respectively. Rates of grade ≥ 3 AEs were 64 (50.4%) of 127 and 48 (38.7%) of 124, respectively. There were no deaths as a result of AEs.",0.5239,47.61,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary endpoints are both PFS. The publication reports PFS as median PFS and a hazard ratio, which are analyses and summaries of the same endpoint rather than a different outcome. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed definition, timeframe, population, or measurement method. The absence of the original registry version prevents confirmation of the earliest prospective wording but does not support an outcome switch. Registry change history was not retrievable, so the current registry entry was used cautiously. The original prospective primary outcome cannot be independently confirmed; no major switch is assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-Free Survival (PFS), reported as stratified median PFS and stratified hazard ratio",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-Free Survival (PFS)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-Free Survival (PFS), stratified median PFS"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:03:08.799741+00:00
NCT02379247_33602685,Phase I: Recommended Phase II Dose (RP2D) of BYL-719 (Alpelisib) + Nab-paclitaxel to be Used in Combination to Treat Advanced HER2-negative Breast Cancer [Time Frame: 12 months] | Phase II: Overall Response Rate (ORR) of Subjects Treated at the Recommended Phase II Dose (RP2D) [Time Frame: 24 months],"A total of 43 patients were enrolled (phase I, n = 13 and phase II, n = 30). A total of 84% had visceral disease and 84% had prior taxane. No dose-limiting toxicities occurred in phase I. RP2D was alpelisib 350 mg daily plus nab-paclitaxel 100 mg/m2 on days 1, 8, and 15. Hyperglycemia (grade 3, 26% and grade 4, 0%), neutropenia (grade 3, 23% and grade 4, 7%), diarrhea (grade 3, 5% and grade 4, 0%), and rash (grade 3, 7% and grade 4, 0%) were the most common adverse events. Among 42 evaluable patients, ORR was 59% (complete response, 7% and partial response, 52%), 21% of whom had response lasting >12 months; median PFS was 8.7 months. A total of 40% of patients demonstrated tumor and/or ctDNA PIK3CA mutation; patients with tumor/ctDNA mutation demonstrated better PFS compared with those without mutation (11.9 vs. 7.5 months; HR, 0.44; P = 0.027). Patients with normal metabolic status had longer PFS compared with prediabetic/diabetic patients (12 vs. 7.5 months; P = 0.014). No pharmacokinetics interactions were detected.",0.745,25.5,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"Both registered primary endpoints are reported in the publication. The reported RP2D corresponds to the registered dose-finding endpoint, and the reported ORR corresponds to the registered Phase II efficacy endpoint; ORR is additionally reconstructable from the reported complete and partial response percentages. Median PFS, safety, pharmacokinetic findings, and biomarker analyses do not replace the registered primary endpoints. The absence of the original registry version prevents historical amendment assessment but does not provide evidence of outcome switching. The original prospective registry version and any amendment history are unavailable. Only the current registry entry can be assessed, so the current wording is treated cautiously and cannot support a major switch determination. Both current registered primary endpoints are reported in the publication.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.94,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Phase I: RP2D of alpelisib 350 mg daily plus nab-paclitaxel 100 mg/m2 on days 1, 8, and 15; Phase II: ORR of 59% among evaluable patients, comprising complete and partial responses",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Recommended Phase II Dose (RP2D) of BYL-719 (alpelisib) plus nab-paclitaxel"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""RP2D was alpelisib 350 mg daily plus nab-paclitaxel 100 mg/m2 on days 1, 8, and 15"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Overall Response Rate (ORR) of subjects treated at the RP2D"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""ORR was 59% among 42 evaluable patients, including complete response 7% and partial response 52%"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:03:24.686327+00:00
NCT01796197_35923923,Percentages of Participants With Pathologic Complete Response [Time Frame: 18 weeks] | Residual Cancer Burden Rate [Time Frame: 18 Weeks],"Twenty-three participants were enrolled, of whom 21 completed surgery following neoadjuvant therapy. Paired longitudinal fresh-frozen tumor samples (D1 and D8) were obtained from all patients. Among the 21 patients who underwent surgery, the pCR and the 4-year disease-free survival were 48% (90% CI 0.29-0.67) and 90% (95% CI 66-97%), respectively. The transcriptional profile of D8 biopsies was found to be more predictive of pCR (AUC = 0.91, 95% CI: 0.7993-1) than the D1 biopsies (AUC = 0.79, 95% CI: 0.5905-0.9822).",0.5542,44.58,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,minor_modification,"The published primary construct, pCR, is clinically equivalent to the registered pCR endpoint. However, the publication explicitly describes a 16-week treatment course, whereas the current registry specifies pCR assessment at 18 weeks, supporting a timeframe modification. RCB is not reported in the provided publication text, but because the original registry version and amendment chronology are unavailable, this omission is not sufficient to assign a major undisclosed switch. The reported disease-free survival and predictive AUC analyses are additional reported outcomes rather than replacements for the pCR construct. Only the current registry entry is available, so the original prospective primary outcome cannot be established. The current entry was used cautiously, and no major switch was assigned solely on the basis of the current wording. Guardrail applied: Endpoint 2: clinical-construct equivalence was not established.",medium,False,True,False,False,definition_or_timeframe_changed,0.65,Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population),modified,Pathologic complete response (pCR) rate after neoadjuvant therapy; 4-year disease-free survival and biopsy transcriptional-profile predictive accuracy were also reported.,"The registry specifies pCR assessment at 18 weeks, whereas the publication describes patients treated with neoadjuvant therapy for 16 weeks before surgery and reports pCR after that treatment.",False,Endpoint 2: clinical-construct equivalence was not established.,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with pathologic complete response (pCR) [18 weeks]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""pCR rate among participants undergoing surgery after 16 weeks of neoadjuvant therapy"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": false, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The registry specifies pCR assessment at 18 weeks, whereas the publication describes patients treated with neoadjuvant therapy for 16 weeks before surgery and reports pCR after that treatment.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome modification (timeframe/definition/population)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Residual cancer burden (RCB) rate [18 weeks]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""No RCB rate reported in the provided publication text"", ""same_construct"": null, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,minor_modification,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:03:54.032142+00:00
NCT01127763_24684785,"Clinical Benefit Rate (Complete Response, Partial Response, and Stable Disease That Lasts More Than 6 Months) [Time Frame: up to 1 year] | Toxicity Profile-Hematological [Time Frame: treatment period (up to 1 year) plus 30 days off treatment] | Toxicity Profile-Non Hematological [Time Frame: treatment period (up to 1 year) plus 30 days off treatment]","We enrolled 25 patients in this study. Median age was 58 years. There were one CR, six PRs, seven SDs and eight PDs (progression of disease). CBR was 36% (95% confidence interval (CI) 21.1 to 57.4%). One SD was achieved in a patient progressing on single agent carboplatin. The median progression free survival (PFS) was 3 months (95% CI 1.6 to 4.6 months) and overall survival (OS) was 16.6 months (95% CI 7.3 months to not reached). There were seven patients (28%) with ≥ grade 3 thrombocytopenia; three (12%) with grade 3 neutropenia (no bleeding/febrile neutropenia) and one (4%) with grade 3 anemia. Greater hematological toxicity was seen in the first seven patients treated with carboplatin AUC5/6. After the amendment for starting dose of carboplatin to AUC 4, the regimen was well tolerated with only one out of 18 patients with grade 3 neutropenia and two patients with grade 3 thrombocytopenia. There was only one case of mucositis.",0.4664,53.36,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication reports the same clinical benefit rate construct as the current registered primary outcome and reports both registered toxicity domains. The timeframe and detailed toxicity assessment windows are not restated in the extracted publication text, but this is missing detail rather than affirmative evidence of modification. Median PFS, the registered secondary outcome, is reported. OS is an additional reported outcome but does not replace or demote the registered primary outcomes. The original prospective registry version and change history were unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously and cannot establish whether these outcomes were the original prespecified outcomes. No major switch is assigned on the basis of the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR), with hematological and non-hematological toxicity reported as primary safety outcomes.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Clinical Benefit Rate (Complete Response, Partial Response, and Stable Disease That Lasts More Than 6 Months) [Time Frame: up to 1 year]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR), reported as 36% based on complete responses, partial responses, and qualifying stable disease."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Toxicity Profile-Hematological [Time Frame: treatment period up to 1 year plus 30 days off treatment]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Hematological toxicity, including grade 3 or higher thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, and anemia."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Toxicity Profile-Non Hematological [Time Frame: treatment period up to 1 year plus 30 days off treatment]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Non-hematological toxicity, including one reported case of mucositis."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:04:13.959695+00:00
NCT01610284_28576675,"Progression Free Survival (PFS) Based on Local Investigator Assessment - Full Analysis Set (FAS) in Full Population, Main Study Cohort and PI3K Unknown Cohort [Time Frame: Date of randomization to the date of first documented tumor progression or death from any cause, whichever occurs first, reported between day of first patient randomized up to approximately 4 years]","Between Sept 7, 2012, and Sept 10, 2014, 1147 patients from 267 centres in 29 countries were randomly assigned to receive buparlisib (n=576) or placebo plus fulvestrant (n=571). In the total patient population (n=1147), median progression-free survival was 6·9 months (95% CI 6·8-7·8) in the buparlisib group versus 5·0 months (4·0-5·2) in the placebo group (hazard ratio [HR] 0·78 [95% CI 0·67-0·89]; one-sided p=0·00021). In patients with known PI3K status (n=851), median progression-free survival was 6·8 months (95% CI 5·0-7·0) in the buparlisib group vs 4·5 months (3·3-5·0) in the placebo group (HR 0·80 [95% CI 0·68-0·94]; one-sided p=0·0033). In PI3K pathway-activated patients (n=372), median progression-free survival was 6·8 months (95% CI 4·9-7·1) in the buparlisib group versus 4·0 months (3·1-5·2) in the placebo group (HR 0·76 [0·60-0·97], one-sided p=0·014). The most common grade 3-4 adverse events in the buparlisib group versus the placebo group were increased alanine aminotransferase (146 [25%] of 573 patients vs six [1%] of 570), increased aspartate aminotransferase (103 [18%] vs 16 [3%]), hyperglycaemia (88 [15%] vs one [<1%]), and rash (45 [8%] vs none). Serious adverse events were reported in 134 (23%) of 573 patients in the buparlisib group compared with 90 [16%] of 570 patients in the placebo group; the most common serious adverse events (affecting ≥2% of patients) were increased alanine aminotransferase (17 [3%] of 573 vs one [<1%] of 570) and increased aspartate aminotransferase (14 [2%] vs one [<1%]). No treatment-related deaths occurred.",0.5742,42.58,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary outcomes are both PFS. Reporting median PFS and hazard ratios, and providing additional PFS analyses in PI3K-defined populations, does not change the endpoint. The publication does not affirmatively report a different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method; non-restatement of registry qualifiers is insufficient to classify a modification or switch. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective primary outcome cannot be verified. The current registry entry was used cautiously and no major switch was inferred from the later wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.96,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS), reported as median PFS in the total randomized patient population, with additional analyses in patients with known PI3K status and PI3K pathway-activated patients.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival (PFS) based on local investigator assessment in the FAS, from randomization to first documented progression or death from any cause, in the full population, main study cohort, and PI3K-unknown cohort."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival (PFS), primarily reported as median PFS in the total patient population."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:04:25.366891+00:00
NCT02896855_32564260,"Progression-Free Survival, as Determined by the Investigator Using Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors Version 1.1 (RECIST v1.1) [Time Frame: From date of randomization until date of PFS event (Median [range] time on study for Arm A vs. Arm B at Primary analysis: 57.14 [3.3-93.3] weeks vs. 59.64 [0.9-90.4] weeks; Final analysis: 145.29 [3.3-225.3] weeks vs. 174.79 [0.9-226.1] weeks)] | Kaplan-Meier Estimate of the Percentage of Participants Event-Free for Progression-Free Survival at 1 to 3 Years, as Determined by the Investigator Using RECIST v1.1 [Time Frame: At 1, 2, and 3 years]","Two hundred forty-three patients were randomized. Median PFS was 14.5 months in the pertuzumab arm (95% confidence interval [CI] 12.5, 18.6) and 12.4 months in the placebo arm (95% CI 10.4, 12.7) in the intention-to-treat population (HR: 0.69 [95% CI 0.49, 0.99]). Objective responses were recorded in 83/105 (79.0%) and 67/97 (69.1%) patients, respectively. Grade ≥ 3 adverse events (70.5% and 69.2%, respectively) and serious adverse events (19.7% and 19.2%, respectively) were similar across both arms. No heart failure cases or symptomatic left ventricular ejection fraction declines were reported.",0.5765,42.35,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary outcomes are both PFS. Reporting median PFS and a hazard ratio rather than repeating every registered timeframe, RECIST qualifier, or event-free percentage does not establish a different endpoint. The publication also reports objective response and safety outcomes, which are registered secondary outcomes rather than a replacement primary endpoint. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed timeframe, definition, population, measurement method, or endpoint hierarchy. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective primary outcome cannot be independently confirmed. The current registry entry specifies PFS as the primary outcome. Because the publication also reports PFS as its primary outcome, the absence of the original registry version does not support an outcome switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-Free Survival (PFS), reported as median PFS in the intention-to-treat population and analyzed using a hazard ratio.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-Free Survival (PFS), investigator-determined using RECIST v1.1, from randomization until PFS event, with event-free estimates at 1, 2, and 3 years."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""PFS, reported as median PFS in the intention-to-treat population: 14.5 months versus 12.4 months, HR 0.69."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:04:38.440450+00:00
NCT03333915_33128299,Phase 1: Number of Participants With Treatment- Emergent Adverse Events (TEAE) and Serious Adverse Events (SAE) [Time Frame: From first dose to within 30 days of last dose of pamiparib (approximately 36 months)] | Phase 1: Number of Participants With Clinically Significant Abnormalities in Physical Examinations and Electrocardiograms (ECGs) [Time Frame: From first dose to within 30 days of last dose of pamiparib (approximately 36 months)] | Phase 2: Objective Response Rate (ORR) in High Grade Ovarian Cancer (HGOC) Both PSOC and PROC as Assessed by Independent Radiology Review Committee (IRC) [Time Frame: Up to approximately 2 years and 8 months],"Nine HGOC and six TNBC patients (N = 15; n = 4, 20 mg; n = 4, 40 mg; n = 7, 60 mg) were enrolled; as of 30 September 2019, one HGOC patient remained on treatment. Seven patients (n = 5, HGOC; n = 2, TNBC) had germline BRCA1/2 mutation (gBRCAmut ); all HGOC patients were resistant/refractory to platinum. Asthenia and nausea (n = 12 each) were the most common treatment-related adverse events (TRAEs). Decreased hemoglobin was the most common grade 3 TRAE (n = 3); no grade ≥4 AEs were observed. No dose-limiting toxicities (DLTs) were reported. Pamiparib plasma exposure was similar to exposure observed in the FIH study after a single-dose administration, albeit slightly higher at steady state. Among 13 RECIST-evaluable patients, two with HGOC (gBRCAmut , n = 1) achieved a confirmed partial response and six with HGOC (gBRCAmut , n = 4) achieved stable disease; all TNBC RECIST-evaluable patients (n = 5) reported progressive disease.",0.629,37.1,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication's principal outcome is phase 1 safety/tolerability, which is clinically concordant with the current phase 1 registered primary safety outcomes. Response findings are additional efficacy results and do not establish replacement of the safety endpoint. Specific physical-examination and ECG details are not present in the supplied text, but this is missing reporting detail rather than affirmative evidence of endpoint modification. Because the original registry version is unavailable, no major switch is inferred from the current entry. Registry change history was unavailable, so the current registry entry cannot be confirmed as the original prospective version. It was used cautiously. The current entry includes phase 1 safety outcomes and a phase 2 HGOC ORR outcome; the publication reports a phase 1 safety/tolerability study with additional response findings. The unavailable original version precludes determining whether any later phase-specific wording was amended before results were known.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.83,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Phase 1 safety and tolerability, including treatment-related adverse events, serious adverse events, dose-limiting toxicities, and overall treatment tolerability; tumor responses were also described as efficacy results.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Phase 1: Number of participants with treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) and serious adverse events (SAEs)."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Safety and tolerability, including treatment-related adverse events; no dose-limiting toxicities were reported and no grade >=4 adverse events were observed."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Phase 1: Number of participants with clinically significant abnormalities in physical examinations and ECGs."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""General safety and tolerability assessment; specific physical-examination and ECG abnormality results were not clearly presented in the supplied publication text."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Phase 2: Objective response rate (ORR) in HGOC assessed by independent radiology review committee."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""RECIST-evaluable HGOC responses, including two confirmed partial responses among eight RECIST-evaluable HGOC patients."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:05:01.185863+00:00
NCT00570921_24327334,Time to Progression [Time Frame: Duration of time start of treatment to time of documented progression or death],"Primary endpoint was time to progression (TTP) and secondary endpoints included objective response rate, clinical benefit rate (CBR), safety, and biomarker correlates",0.5677,43.23,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered current primary endpoint and the published primary endpoint are the same clinical construct and are described with the same terminology. The publication also identifies objective response rate and clinical benefit rate as secondary endpoints, consistent with the registry. No explicit evidence demonstrates a changed timeframe, definition, population, measurement method, replacement, or demotion of TTP. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and only the current registry entry can be assessed. The current registered primary endpoint is TTP, and the publication explicitly identifies TTP as the primary endpoint. Because registry chronology cannot be verified, the current wording is treated cautiously; there is no basis for inferring a major switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.99,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Time to progression (TTP),,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Time to Progression (TTP): duration from start of treatment to documented progression or death"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Time to progression (TTP)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:05:11.321238+00:00
NCT01196052_25713436,"Percentage of Participants With a Cardiac Event Within 12 Weeks After the Start of Trastuzumab Emtansine Treatment [Time Frame: Baseline to 12 weeks after the start of trastuzumab emtansine treatment] | Adverse Events, LVEF Function, and Deaths [Time Frame: From the start to the end of trastuzumab emtansine treatment (up to 51 weeks)]","Median follow-up was 24.6 months. No prespecified cardiac events or symptomatic congestive heart failures were reported. Four patients (2.7%) had asymptomatic LVEF declines (≥ 10 percentage points from baseline to LVEF < 50%), leading to T-DM1 discontinuation in one patient. Of 148 patients who received ≥ one cycle of T-DM1, 82.4% completed the planned 1-year duration of HER2-directed therapy. During T-DM1 treatment, 38.5% and 2.7% of patients experienced grade 3 and 4 adverse events, respectively. Approximately 95% of patients receiving T-DM1 plus radiotherapy completed ≥ 95% of the planned radiation dose with delay ≤ 5 days.",0.6233,37.67,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication reports the same overall cardiac safety, LVEF, adverse-event, and treatment-completion constructs represented in the available current primary outcomes. The abstract does not restate every registered timeframe, component, or assessment detail, but it provides no affirmative evidence of a different primary endpoint, replacement, or demotion. Missing or separately unreported secondary outcomes do not establish primary outcome switching. No registry change history was available. The current registry entry was therefore treated cautiously and could not be assumed to represent the original prospective registration. A later registry amendment or its timing relative to results cannot be established.",medium,True,True,False,False,,0.78,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Safety, cardiac tolerability, and treatment feasibility outcomes, including cardiac events, LVEF declines, adverse events, and completion of planned trastuzumab emtansine treatment.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants with a cardiac event within 12 weeks after the start of trastuzumab emtansine treatment."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Prespecified cardiac events or symptomatic congestive heart failure, with reported asymptomatic LVEF declines."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Adverse events, LVEF function, and deaths from the start to the end of trastuzumab emtansine treatment, up to 51 weeks."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Adverse events during T-DM1 treatment, LVEF declines, and treatment completion over approximately 1 year."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:05:31.902962+00:00
NCT02489448_32709714,Pathologic Complete Response (pCR) [Time Frame: Up to 26 weeks],"In patients with pCR, PD-L1 expression was significantly higher in tumor cells, in CD68+ cells and in the stroma compared with patients non-pCR. There was no difference in the amount of CD68+ cells in the tumor or stromal compartments between cases with pCR and non-pCR.",0.5724,42.76,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication's principal clinical outcome is pCR, which is the same endpoint as the current registered primary outcome. The PD-L1 expression findings are analyses associated with pCR and do not constitute replacement of the registered endpoint. The publication does not explicitly report a different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. Only the current registry entry was available; the original prospective registry version and any amendments could not be established. The current registered primary outcome was therefore used cautiously. No major switch is supported by the available evidence.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.9,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Pathologic complete response (pCR),,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Pathologic Complete Response (pCR)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Pathologic complete response (pCR)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:05:42.452543+00:00
NCT00789581_28508185,Disease-free Survival [Time Frame: up to 5.25 years (63 months)],"614 patients were randomized: 306 to AC/ixabepilone and 308 to AC/paclitaxel. At a median follow-up of 48 months, 59 patients had relapsed (AC/ixabepilone, 29; AC/paclitaxel, 30). The median time from diagnosis to relapse was 20.8 months. The 5-year disease-free survival (DFS) rates of the two groups were similar [HR 0.92; ixabepilone 87.1% (95% CI 82.6-90.5) vs. paclitaxel 84.7% (95% CI 79.7-88.6)]. The estimated 5-year overall survival (OS) rates were also similar [HR 1.1; ixabepilone 89.7% (95% CI 85.5-92.7) vs. paclitaxel 89.6% (95% CI 85.0-92.9)]. Peripheral neuropathy was the most common grade 3/4 event. Dose reductions and treatment discontinuations occurred more frequently during paclitaxel treatment.",0.3908,60.92,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered current primary endpoint, disease-free survival, is reported as the published primary endpoint. The publication also reports overall survival, which corresponds to the registered secondary outcome. The wording and stated horizon differ slightly, but there is no affirmative evidence of a materially different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. The original prospective registry outcome is unavailable, so no stronger inference about registry amendments is warranted. The original prospective registry version and change history were unavailable. The current registry lists disease-free survival as the primary outcome and overall survival as a secondary outcome. Because the timing and content of any registry amendments cannot be established, the current entry was interpreted cautiously and was not used to infer a switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.92,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,5-year disease-free survival (DFS),,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Disease-free Survival, up to 5.25 years (63 months)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""5-year disease-free survival (DFS)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:05:54.010538+00:00
NCT04588298_41628308,Percentage Change From Baseline in Estrogen Receptor (ER) Expression Between Pre- and On-treatment Tumour Samples (Primary Analysis) [Time Frame: Baseline (Screening Day -21 to 1) to Biopsy day (Days 5-7 [Stage 1 and 2] or Days 12-15 [Stage 3])] | Percentage Change From Baseline in ER Expression Between Pre- and On-treatment Tumour Samples (Sensitivity Analysis) [Time Frame: Baseline (Screening Day -21 to 1) to Biopsy Day (Days 5-7 [Stage 1 and 2] or Days 12-15 [Stage 3])],"ER expression was reduced by approximately 65% for all camizestrant doses, regardless of treatment duration. Reduction in Ki67 expression was greater after 12-15 days of camizestrant treatment, compared with 5-7 days. Exploratory analyses including mass spectrometry and IHC image analysis aligned with IHC H-score, indicating equivalent, maximal effects of all tested doses of camizestrant on ER expression and activity and Ki67 expression. Of those participants receiving camizestrant 75 mg once daily, 90%-100% reported no treatment-emergent adverse events across all preferred terms; of those reported, all were Grade 1 except one participant with Grade 2 upper respiratory tract infection, not considered related to camizestrant.",0.5016,49.84,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary construct is change in ER expression between pre- and on-treatment tumour samples. The publication reports the same ER expression reduction endpoint across the registered biopsy durations. Ki-67, adverse events, and exploratory assay analyses do not replace the ER primary endpoint; Ki-67 and adverse events were registered secondary outcomes, and the assay analyses are additional analyses rather than a new primary endpoint. No affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed timeframe, definition, population, measurement method, or endpoint hierarchy. No registry change history is available, so the original prospective outcome cannot be verified and the current registry entry must be interpreted cautiously. The current registered primary outcome is ER expression change, which corresponds to the ER expression reduction reported as the publication's primary outcome. The unavailable original version does not provide evidence for a switch.",high,True,True,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Reduction in ER expression between pre- and on-treatment tumour samples, reported as approximately 65% after camizestrant treatment, regardless of treatment duration.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage Change From Baseline in ER Expression Between Pre- and On-treatment Tumour Samples (Primary Analysis), baseline to biopsy day at Days 5-7 or Days 12-15."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Approximately 65% reduction in ER expression after 5-7 or 12-15 days of camizestrant treatment."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage Change From Baseline in ER Expression Between Pre- and On-treatment Tumour Samples (Sensitivity Analysis), baseline to biopsy day at Days 5-7 or Days 12-15."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Approximately 65% reduction in ER expression after 5-7 or 12-15 days of camizestrant treatment."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:06:13.960479+00:00
NCT02876302_38297352,Biologic Response To 7-Day Run-In Window Phase Treatment of Ruxolitinib Alone or Ruxolitinib Plus Paclitaxel [Time Frame: 7 days],"Overall, 23 patients were enrolled, of whom 21 completed preoperative therapy. Two patients achieved pCR (8.7%). pSTAT3 and IL-6/JAK/STAT3 signaling decreased in post-run-in biopsies of RUX-treated samples, while sustained treatment with RUX + PAC upregulated IL-6/JAK/STAT3 signaling compared to RUX alone. Both treatments decreased GZMB+ T cells implying immune suppression. RUX alone effectively inhibited JAK/STAT3 signaling but its combination with PAC led to incomplete inhibition. The immune suppressive effects of RUX alone and in combination may negate its growth inhibitory effects on cancer cells.",0.6113,38.87,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary address the same biologic-response construct during the 7-day run-in period. The publication also reports pCR and other biologic findings, but pCR was a registered secondary outcome and does not replace the registered biologic-response primary. No affirmative evidence shows a changed timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. Registry change history was unavailable. The current registry entry was therefore used provisionally, and no major switch was inferred from the current wording alone. The current registered primary is a 7-day biologic-response endpoint, which corresponds to the publication's reported changes in pSTAT3 and IL-6/JAK/STAT3 signaling after run-in treatment.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.86,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Biologic response to 7-day run-in treatment, assessed by changes in pSTAT3 and IL-6/JAK/STAT3 signaling in post-run-in biopsies",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Biologic Response To 7-Day Run-In Window Phase Treatment of Ruxolitinib Alone or Ruxolitinib Plus Paclitaxel [Time Frame: 7 days]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Changes in pSTAT3 and IL-6/JAK/STAT3 signaling after the 7-day run-in treatment with ruxolitinib alone or ruxolitinib plus paclitaxel"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:06:26.245396+00:00
NCT03901339_36027558,Progression-Free Survival (PFS) by Blinded Independent Central Review (BICR) Assessment [Time Frame: Up to 42.8 months],"Primary end point was met with a 34% reduction in risk of progression or death (hazard ratio, 0",0.5121,48.79,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary outcome and published primary outcome are both PFS. The publication explicitly reports progression or death, which is clinically consistent with PFS, and reports the endpoint as the primary endpoint. The excerpt does not restate the BICR method or registered timeframe, but this is missing detail rather than affirmative evidence of a different endpoint. Reported OS and safety findings are secondary outcomes and do not indicate outcome switching. The original prospective registry version is unavailable and no registry change history can be retrieved. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously. Because the publication reports PFS as its primary endpoint, the later wording does not provide evidence of a switch.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Progression-free survival (PFS), defined by progression or death, reported as the primary endpoint",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression-Free Survival (PFS) by Blinded Independent Central Review (BICR) Assessment"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression-free survival (PFS), with progression or death as events"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:06:39.832950+00:00
NCT03674112_34147014,"Percentage of Participants by Their Preferred Method of Pertuzumab and Trastuzumab Administration, as Assessed in Question 1 of the Patient Preference Questionnaire (PPQ) [Time Frame: Cycle 6 Day 1 (each cycle is 21 days)]","One hundred and sixty patients were randomised (cut-off: 24 February 2020); 136 (85.0%, 95% confidence interval: 78.5-90.2%) preferred SC; 22 (13.8%) preferred IV; 2 (1.3%) had no preference. The main reasons for SC preference were reduced clinic time (n = 119) and comfort during administration (n = 73). One hundred and forty-one patients (88.1%) were very satisfied/satisfied with SC injection versus 108 (67.5%) with IV infusion; 86.9% chose PH FDC SC continuation. HCP perceptions of median patient treatment room time ranged from 33.0-50.0 min with SC and 130.0-300.0 min with IV. Most adverse events (AEs) were grade 1/2 (no 4/5s); serious AE rates were low. AE rates before and after switching were similar (cycles 1-3 IV → cycles 4-6 SC: 77.5% → 72.5%; cycles 1-3 SC → cycles 4-6 IV: 77.5% → 63.8%).",0.5007,49.93,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered and published primary outcomes assess the same clinical construct: participant preference for SC versus IV pertuzumab and trastuzumab administration. The publication reports the registered preference outcome, while also reporting prespecified secondary outcomes such as reasons for preference, satisfaction, continuation choice, healthcare-professional perceptions, and safety. The supplied publication text does not restate the registered Cycle 6 Day 1 timeframe or all PPQ qualifiers, but it provides no affirmative evidence of a different timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method. No registry change history or original prospective version was available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously, and no major switch was assigned based on later wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.97,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Participant preference for subcutaneous pertuzumab and trastuzumab fixed-dose combination (PH FDC SC) versus intravenous pertuzumab plus trastuzumab (P+H IV), with 85.0% preferring SC, 13.8% preferring IV, and 1.3% having no preference.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants by their preferred method of pertuzumab and trastuzumab administration, assessed using Question 1 of the PPQ at Cycle 6 Day 1."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Percentage of participants preferring PH FDC SC, P+H IV, or having no preference."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:06:56.582999+00:00
NCT03193853_37491309,"Efficacy (Objective Response Rate) [Time Frame: Through study completion, up to 2 years 8 months]","With cisplatin/nab paclitaxel (cis/nab pac) therapy post PIKTOR, 3 patients had clinical benefit (1 partial response (PR) and 2 stable disease (SD) ≥ 6 months) and continued to have durable benefit in progression-free survival with pembrolizumab post-cis/nab pac for 1.2, 2, and 3.6 years. Their post-PIKTOR metastatic tissue displayed decreased mismatch repair (MMR), increased tumor mutation burden, and significantly lower levels of 53BP1, DAG Lipase β, GCN2, AKT Ser473, and PKCzeta Thr410/403 compared to pre-PIKTOR tumor tissue.",0.4624,53.76,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,major_switch,"Objective Response Rate and clinical benefit/disease control are not clinically equivalent because the published endpoint includes prolonged stable disease, which is not an objective response. The registered primary endpoint is not explicitly reported as ORR in the provided publication text. However, because the original prospective registry version is unavailable and the current registry entry must be treated cautiously, the evidence supports a moderate rather than major switch classification. Only the current registry entry was available, specifying Objective Response Rate as the primary outcome. The original prospective registry version and any amendments could not be established; therefore, a major switch is not assigned solely on the basis of the current wording.",medium,False,True,False,True,primary_replaced | prespecified_omitted,0.78,Outcome switch — major / undisclosed,different,"Clinical benefit/disease control with subsequent cisplatin/nab-paclitaxel, defined in the reported patients as partial response or stable disease for at least 6 months, with durable progression-free survival","The registry primary endpoint is Objective Response Rate. | The publication presents clinical benefit/disease control that includes stable disease lasting at least 6 months, whereas stable disease is not part of Objective Response Rate.",False,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective Response Rate"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Clinical benefit/disease control, including 1 partial response and 2 cases of stable disease lasting at least 6 months, with durable progression-free survival"", ""same_construct"": false, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": false, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": false, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": false, ""actual_change_evidence"": [""The registry primary endpoint is Objective Response Rate."", ""The publication presents clinical benefit/disease control that includes stable disease lasting at least 6 months, whereas stable disease is not part of Objective Response Rate.""], ""classification_label"": ""Outcome switch — major / undisclosed""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,major_switch,False,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:07:09.342183+00:00
NCT01277757_31277699,"Number of Participants With Response Defined Using Response Evaluation Criteria In Solid Tumors (RECIST) [Time Frame: Up to 3 weeks after completion of study treatment, for up to 1 year] | Number of Participants With Objective Response [Time Frame: 4 weeks following beginning treatment, repeat confirmation 4-6 weeks following response, up to 1 year]","Twenty-seven patients received MK-2206. Eighteen patients were enrolled into the PIK3CA/AKT1 mutation arm (cohort A): 13 had PIK3CA mutations, four had AKT1 mutations, and one had a PIK3CA mutation as well as PTEN loss. ORR and 6 m PFS were both 5.6% (1/18), with one patient with HR+ breast cancer and a PIK3CA E542K mutation experiencing a partial response (on treatment for 36 weeks). Nine patients were enrolled on the PTEN loss/mutation arm (cohort B). ORR was 0% and 6 m PFS was 11% (1/9), observed in a patient with triple-negative breast cancer and PTEN loss. The study was stopped early due to futility. The most common adverse events were fatigue (48%) and rash (44%). On pre-treatment biopsy, PIK3CA and AKT1 mutation status was concordant with archival tissue testing. However, two patients with PTEN loss based on archival testing had PTEN expression on the pre-treatment biopsy. MK-2206 treatment was associated with a significant decline in pAKT S473 and pAKT T308 and PI3K activation score in PBMC and PRPs, but not in tumor biopsies. By IHC, there was no significant decrease in median pAKT S473 or Ki-67 staining, but a drop was observed in both responders.",0.4595,54.05,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The publication reports ORR, which is clinically equivalent to the registered RECIST-defined response and objective-response primary outcomes. The registered confirmation schedule and other qualifiers are not fully restated in the provided text, but there is no affirmative evidence that the timeframe, definition, population, or measurement method changed. Six-month PFS is a registered secondary outcome, not evidence of primary endpoint switching. Registry change history is unavailable, so the original prospective primary endpoint cannot be established. The current registry entry was used cautiously. The publication's ORR is clinically equivalent to the registered response/objective-response outcomes, and no major switch is assigned based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.93,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Objective response rate (ORR), with 6-month progression-free survival also reported.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Response Defined Using RECIST"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Objective response rate (ORR), reported as 1/18 in cohort A and 0/9 in cohort B"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Objective Response, with response confirmation 4–6 weeks after response"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Objective response rate (ORR), reported as 1/18 in cohort A and 0/9 in cohort B"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:07:22.434445+00:00
NCT04148911_42330688,Percentage of Participants With Treatment-emergent Grade ≥3 Adverse Events (AEs) [Time Frame: Up to 60 months] | Percentage of Participants With Treatment-emergent Grade ≥2 Immune-mediated AEs (imAEs) [Time Frame: Up to 60 months],"Among 182 treated patients, five had CNS metastases and three had ECOG PS ≥ 2. Patients received a median of six treatment cycles (range 1-63); 7% were treated for >3 years. Grade ≥2 immune-mediated AEs occurred in 12% of patients (95% CI 8%-18%) and grade ≥3 AEs in 47% (95% CI 39%-54%). There were no treatment-related deaths. Median PFS was 7.4 (95% CI 5.6-10.6) months (>2 years in 27 patients [15%]) and median overall survival was 27.0 (95% CI 22.0-33.8) months. Concordance between local and central PD-L1 testing was 67% (65/97). Median PFS was >11 months in patients with centrally confirmed PD-L1-positive status.",0.5504,44.96,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"Both current registered primary safety endpoints are reported in the publication using clinically equivalent definitions. The publication does not restate the registered up-to-60-month timeframe, but this absence is not evidence of a changed timeframe. PFS and OS are registered secondary outcomes, and their reporting does not replace or switch the primary safety endpoints. No registry change history or original prospective registration was available. The current registry entry was used cautiously, and no major switch was inferred from the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.93,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Treatment-emergent Grade ≥3 AEs and Grade ≥2 immune-mediated AEs, reported as percentages among 182 treated patients",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With Treatment-emergent Grade ≥3 Adverse Events (AEs) [Time Frame: Up to 60 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Grade ≥3 AEs occurred in 47% of patients"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Percentage of Participants With Treatment-emergent Grade ≥2 Immune-mediated AEs (imAEs) [Time Frame: Up to 60 months]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Grade ≥2 immune-mediated AEs occurred in 12% of patients"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:07:39.012805+00:00
NCT00445458_23632474,"Dose Limiting Toxicity Incidence of Neratinib in Combination With Paclitaxel [Time Frame: From first dose date through day 28] | Maximum Tolerated Dose [Time Frame: From first dose date through day 28.] | Objective Response Rate [Time Frame: From first dose date to progression or last tumor assessment, up to 140 weeks]","Eight patients were included in the dose-escalation study; no dose-limiting toxicities were observed, and an MTD of oral neratinib 240 mg once daily plus intravenous paclitaxel 80 mg m(-2) on days 1, 8, and 15 of each 28-day cycle was determined. A total of 102 patients with HER2-positive breast cancer were enrolled in part 2. The overall median treatment duration was 47.9 weeks (range: 0.1-147.3 weeks). Common treatment-emergent adverse events (all grades/grade ≥3) included diarrhoea (92%/29%; none grade 4), peripheral sensory neuropathy (51%/3%), neutropenia (50%/20%), alopecia (46%/0%), leukopenia (41%/18%), anaemia (37%/8%), and nausea (34%/1%). Three (3%) patients discontinued treatment due to an adverse event (mouth ulceration, left ventricular ejection fraction reduction, and acute renal failure). Among the 99 evaluable patients in part 2 of the study, the overall response rate (ORR) was 73% (95% confidence interval (CI): 62.9-81.2%), including 7 (7%) patients who achieved a complete response; an additional 9 (9%) patients achieved stable disease for at least 24 weeks. ORR was 71% among patients with 0/1 prior chemotherapy regimen for metastatic disease and no prior lapatinib, and 77% among those with 2/3 prior chemotherapy regimens for metastatic disease with prior lapatinib permitted. Kaplan-Meier median progression-free survival was 57.0 weeks (95% CI: 47.7-81.6 weeks). Pharmacokinetic analyses indicated no interaction between neratinib and paclitaxel.",0.7604,23.96,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"All three currently registered primary outcomes are reported in the publication and retain the same clinical constructs. The publication additionally reports progression-free survival, adverse events, stable disease, and pharmacokinetic findings, but these do not replace or modify the registered primary endpoints. Timeframe and detailed endpoint definitions are not restated in the provided publication text, which is insufficient evidence of a change. Only the current registry entry is available; the original prospective version and any amendment timing cannot be established. The current primary outcomes were therefore used cautiously and no switch was inferred from later wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.88,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Dose-limiting toxicity incidence, maximum tolerated dose, and objective response rate for neratinib plus paclitaxel.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Dose-limiting toxicity incidence of neratinib in combination with paclitaxel, from first dose through day 28."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Dose-limiting toxicity incidence; no dose-limiting toxicities were observed in the dose-escalation study."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Maximum tolerated dose of neratinib in combination with paclitaxel, from first dose through day 28."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Maximum tolerated dose; an MTD of oral neratinib 240 mg once daily plus intravenous paclitaxel 80 mg/m2 on days 1, 8, and 15 of each 28-day cycle was determined."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}, {""registered_endpoint"": ""Objective response rate, from first dose to progression or last tumor assessment, up to 140 weeks."", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Overall response rate; ORR was 73% among 99 evaluable patients in part 2."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:07:56.443580+00:00
NCT00553410_29158011,"Disease-free Survival (DFS) [Time Frame: 5-year estimates, reported at a median follow-up of 60 months]","Between Dec 5, 2007, and Oct 8, 2012, 4884 women were enrolled and randomised after exclusion of patients at a non-adherent centre, found to have inadequate documentation of informed consent, immediately withdrew consent, or randomly assigned to intervention groups in error. 4851 women comprised the intention-to-treat population that compared extended intermittent letrozole use (n=2425) with continuous letrozole use (n=2426). After a median follow-up of 60 months (IQR 53-72), disease-free survival was 85·8% (95% CI 84·2-87·2) in the intermittent letrozole group compared with 87·5% (86·0-88·8) in the continuous letrozole group (hazard ratio 1·08, 95% CI 0·93-1·26; p=0·31). Adverse events were reported as expected and were similar between the two groups. The most common grade 3-5 adverse events were hypertension (584 [24%] of 2417 in the intermittent letrozole group vs 517 [21%] of 2411 in the continuous letrozole group) and arthralgia (136 [6%] vs 151 [6%]). 54 patients (24 [1%] in the intermittent letrozole group and 30 [1%] in the continuous letrozole group) had grade 3-5 CNS cerebrovascular ischaemia, 16 (nine [<1%] vs seven [<1%]) had grade 3-5 CNS haemorrhage, and 40 (19 [1%] vs 21 [1%]) had grade 3-5 cardiac ischaemia. In total, 23 (<1%) of 4851 patients died while on trial treatment (13 [<1%] of 2417 patients in the intermittent letrozole group vs ten [<1%] of 2411 in the continuous letrozole group).",0.4058,59.42,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered current primary endpoint and the published primary endpoint are both disease-free survival, assessed at the same stated follow-up timeframe. The publication reports the registered endpoint directly; the different letrozole schedules represent treatment comparisons, not a change in outcome. The unavailability of the original registry version creates chronology uncertainty but does not provide evidence of outcome switching. No registry change history or original prospective primary outcome is available. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously; its DFS wording matches the published primary outcome, and no major switch is inferred from the absence of the original version.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.98,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Disease-free survival at a median follow-up of 60 months,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Disease-free Survival (DFS), 5-year estimates reported at a median follow-up of 60 months"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Disease-free survival reported after a median follow-up of 60 months"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:08:07.721264+00:00
NCT02091960_33591468,"Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR) [Time Frame: Tumor assessments were performed every 8 weeks through week 49, and then every 12 weeks thereafter until disease progression or death; the median duration of treatment was 70 days, and the maximum was 660 days.]","Overall, 103 women were enrolled [median age 60 years (range 34-83)]; 62% had received ≥ 3 lines of prior anti-HER2 therapy. CBR24, comprising patients with confirmed partial responses (5%) and durable stable disease at 24 weeks (19%), was 24% in the efficacy evaluable set (n = 89). CBR24 did not seem related to AR-expression levels or hormone receptor status. Median PFS was 3.4 months (95% confidence interval 2.0-3.8). Overall, 97 (94%) patients experienced treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), with fatigue most common (34%). Dyspnea (4%) and malignant neoplasm progression (3%) were the only TEAEs grade ≥ 3 reported in ≥ 3 patients. 22 patients (21%) reported serious TEAEs. Four patients (4%) experienced fatal, non-drug-related TEAEs.",0.6059,39.41,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,concordant,"The registered primary and published primary assess the same clinical construct, clinical benefit rate. The publication specifies assessment at 24 weeks, while the available current registry entry provides an assessment schedule but does not clearly state a conflicting endpoint timeframe or definition. The registered CBR is therefore treated as reported as CBR24, and no affirmative evidence demonstrates a changed endpoint. Missing prespecified secondary outcomes do not constitute primary outcome switching. No registry change history is available, and the original prospective primary outcome cannot be confirmed. The current registry entry was therefore used cautiously. The available evidence does not support assigning a switch based on the current wording alone.",high,True,False,False,False,,0.9,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Clinical Benefit Rate at 24 weeks (CBR24), comprising confirmed partial responses and durable stable disease at 24 weeks; CBR24 was 24% in the efficacy evaluable set.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Clinical Benefit Rate (CBR)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Clinical Benefit Rate at 24 weeks (CBR24)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": null, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,concordant,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:08:19.943775+00:00
NCT01194440_29752687,Number of Participants With Aromatase Inhibitor Associated Musculoskeletal Symptoms (AIMSS) [Time Frame: 12 months],"From 2011 to 2013, 59 postmenopausal women enrolled in ZAP trial. All 59 (100%) women received baseline and 52 (88%) received 6-month zoledronic acid, and had similar characteristics to historical controls from the ELPh trial (n = 206). Cumulatively during the first year of AI, 37 and 67% of ZAP and ELPh participants reported AIMSS (p < 0.001), respectively. Within the ZAP trial, we did not observe significant changes in other PROs; however, we report improvements in bone mineral density.",0.5855,41.45,llm,gpt-5.6-luna,additional_outcome,"The registered primary endpoint and published primary endpoint are the same clinical construct and materially equivalent timeframe. The publication compares ZAP participants with historical ELPh controls, but this changes the comparison rather than the endpoint. Bone mineral density is an additional outcome that is transparently mentioned while the registered AIMSS endpoint remains reported. Prespecified secondary outcomes are not clearly reported in the provided publication text, but their absence does not constitute primary outcome switching. Only the current registry entry was available; the original prospective primary outcome and any amendment history could not be established. The current primary outcome was therefore used cautiously, and no major switch was assigned based on the later wording alone.",high,True,False,True,False,unregistered_added_disclosed,0.9,Additional outcome (disclosed exploratory),clinically equivalent,Cumulative incidence/number of participants reporting aromatase inhibitor-associated musculoskeletal symptoms (AIMSS) during the first year of aromatase inhibitor therapy,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants With Aromatase Inhibitor Associated Musculoskeletal Symptoms (AIMSS) at 12 months"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Participants reporting AIMSS cumulatively during the first year of aromatase inhibitor therapy"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": null, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": true, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": true, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Additional outcome (disclosed exploratory)""}]",auto_accepted,confirm,additional_outcome,True,,AUTO,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T21:08:35.491206+00:00
